/* Generated from content/case-studies.json and content/case-study-stories.json. */
window.CASE_STUDIES = {"onespace":{"slug":"onespace","name":"OneSpace","kind":"ux","headline":"Finding a class file should be straightforward.","summary":"A research-led communication concept for students and teachers navigating classes, files, and announcements across disconnected apps.","role":"UX research, problem framing, flows & interface design","status":"Design challenge concept","context":"Microsoft Design Challenge · Education communication","facts":[{"value":"127","label":"survey responses"},{"value":"5+","label":"one-to-one interviews"},{"value":"Mobile","label":"primary prototype"}],"problem":"During the shift to online classes, students were being asked to follow different teachers across different communication tools. My starting question was whether bringing communication into one place could make collaboration easier. The more useful question became: how does a student find the right resource when they need it?","process":["I began with more than five one-to-one interviews and a virtual discussion with students from different colleges, then used a ten-question survey answered by 127 people. The interviews exposed an adoption mismatch: teachers chose tools they found comfortable, while students inherited the work of keeping up with all of them.","The survey complicated my original assumption. The deck reports that more than half of respondents did not mind using multiple apps, while about 14% found it frustrating. Interest in an all-in-one tool was therefore a signal to explore, rather than proof that every communication tool needed replacing. I translated the findings into a student persona, four problem areas, information architecture, wireframes, and high-fidelity screens."],"decisions":[{"title":"Organise around a class, then its conversations","choice":"The concept uses spaces and categorised channels, with calls accessible from the chat context.","reason":"A class provides a recognisable place to return to; students should not have to remember which external app a teacher chose.","tradeoff":"This would require institutional participation. A cleaner student interface alone cannot solve fragmented adoption."},{"title":"Give files and announcements their own home","choice":"I separated a unified file store and an announcements centre from the conversation stream.","reason":"These are the items students need to retrieve later. Their usefulness should not depend on scrolling back through chat.","tradeoff":"Separating destinations adds navigation; useful defaults and clear links back to the class would be essential."},{"title":"Treat access as part of the product scope","choice":"The prototype explores language support and preferences alongside the core communication screens.","reason":"The interviews describe uneven comfort with technology. Familiar structures and understandable language matter as much as consolidating features.","tradeoff":"The deck also explores watch support and parental controls. Today I would defer those until the core retrieval journey had been tested."}],"outcome":"I designed spaces for classes, channels for conversation, and separate places for files and announcements. The prototype covers the wider communication flow, but those two destinations are the part I would carry into a first release. The project stopped at a prototype; usability testing was the next step.","reflection":"The survey made me question my original all-in-one idea. Being willing to try a new app did not mean students needed every tool replaced. I would start with finding a file and catching an announcement, then test whether those tasks are enough to justify another app.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask students to retrieve an assignment, identify the latest announcement, and join the correct class; compare task success and recovery with their current workflow.","Proposed scope: pilot one class before adding institution-wide migration, parental controls, or a second device. Include teacher setup effort in the evaluation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 2–7","detail":"Process, problem statement, interviews, survey and problem identification."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 11–17","detail":"Information architecture, wireframes, final screens and the proposed usability study."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p05.webp","caption":"The original survey: a useful counterpoint to my initial assumption about app overload.","alt":"Original OneSpace survey slide with response charts.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p14.webp","caption":"Files and announcements become explicit destinations instead of getting buried in chat.","alt":"Original OneSpace file store, announcements and profile screens.","width":1600,"height":900}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/onespace.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":3,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Finding a class file [[should be straightforward.]]","scene":"Picture a student getting ready for an online class. The conversation is in one app, the link in another, and the assignment somewhere further back. Before learning can begin, there is a search to finish.","question":"Would putting everything into one app actually solve the problem?","insight":"More than half of the survey respondents were [[fine with using multiple apps]]. That changed the question: the stronger opportunity was making class resources easier to retrieve.","pivot":"I organised the concept around a dependable place to return to: the class, its conversations, and the resources attached to it.","takeaway":"A useful home for information matters more than an all-in-one promise.","journey":[{"title":"Find the class","detail":"A recognisable space becomes the starting point."},{"title":"Find the context","detail":"Channels keep the conversation attached to its class."},{"title":"Retrieve what matters","detail":"Files and announcements have their own destinations."}],"decisionLeads":["First, give the student somewhere to return.","Then, design for the moment after a message is sent.","Finally, decide how much the first release should do."],"annotations":[{"title":"Files outlive the chat","detail":"A dedicated file store supports later retrieval."},{"title":"Announcements deserve a signal","detail":"Important class information gets a destination of its own."},{"title":"Preferences stay close","detail":"The prototype explores personal settings alongside the core flow."}],"evidenceTag":"Original survey · 127 responses","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Original research persona","label":"Vivek · student","context":"An engineering student attending online classes across tools chosen by different teachers.","need":"Retrieve class resources when they are needed.","friction":"Resources scattered across apps, limited phone storage, and difficulty asking for help.","implication":"Give the class and its resources a predictable home."}},"theme":{"layout":"collaboration","mode":"light","paper":"#f7fbff","ink":"#173447","dim":"#53697d","line":"#cbdfe9","accent":"#087e88","wash":"#dcf3f4","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Class confusion","process":"What the research changed","decisions":"Designing a dependable home","outcome":"The communication concept","reflection":"The next research question"},"decisionHeading":"Keep class resources easy to find.","resultHeading":"A class, with a place for its resources."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/onespace/p14.webp","alt":"Original OneSpace file store, announcements and profile screens.","caption":"The original screens give files and announcements destinations beyond the conversation stream.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf","focus":[{"title":"Retrieve","detail":"Files have a searchable destination."},{"title":"Notice","detail":"Announcements have a separate reading surface."},{"title":"Personalise","detail":"Preferences remain close to the core experience."}],"width":1600,"height":900,"details":[{"decision":1,"x":95,"y":190,"width":840,"height":610,"caption":"Files and announcements each have a dedicated destination in the original prototype.","alt":"Detail of the original OneSpace file store and announcements screens."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"umatter","name":"uMatter","headline":"A clearer first step towards professional support."}},"umatter":{"slug":"umatter","name":"uMatter","kind":"ux","headline":"A clearer first step towards professional support.","summary":"A 14-hour team challenge exploring a more approachable route from mental-health awareness to finding and booking professional support.","role":"Team lead · UX research & design","status":"Team design challenge concept","context":"Team Elementors · Pixel Perfect UI/UX Challenge","facts":[{"value":"14h","label":"challenge window"},{"value":"65","label":"survey responses"},{"value":"3","label":"team members"}],"problem":"The challenge brief focused on making access to mental-health support more efficient. I led the UX research and design contribution for Team Elementors, working with Pratik Manjrekar on UI design and Nidhi S Kamath on UI and graphic design. Our task was to turn a broad and sensitive brief into a coherent mobile journey within fourteen hours.","process":["We used a shortened Double Diamond process to divide research, structure, and interface work. Our ten-question survey received 65 responses. The deck reports that almost half wanted professional help while remaining anonymous, and almost 89% had never visited a therapist. Those responses guided the concept toward privacy expectations and an approachable first step.","We supplemented the survey with article research, assembled a persona, mapped the flow, and progressed from wireframes to high-fidelity screens. The sample was exploratory: the deck does not document recruitment, a representative population, or a controlled evaluation. I treat its percentages as directional responses from this sample."],"decisions":[{"title":"Build a path to support, rather than promising diagnosis","choice":"The concept connects expert discovery, an expert profile, appointment selection, and payment.","reason":"Breaking access into visible steps gives a person a clearer idea of what happens after they decide to seek help.","tradeoff":"Although the challenge brief used the word diagnosis, these screens do not demonstrate clinical diagnosis or treatment effectiveness."},{"title":"Put trust information before commitment","choice":"Expert detail and review-based trust cues sit before calendar booking and payment in the documented journey.","reason":"A person needs to understand who they are approaching before being asked to commit time or money.","tradeoff":"Ratings alone do not establish professional competence. Credential verification would need its own operational process."},{"title":"Explore lower-pressure ways to engage","choice":"The team added awareness content, stories, and lighter activities alongside the booking journey.","reason":"Not every visitor is ready to book immediately. The concept offers a way to explore the topic before taking that step.","tradeoff":"More destinations can dilute the primary route to professional support; I would test whether they help or distract."}],"outcome":"We completed the mobile concept within the fourteen-hour challenge, from onboarding and expert profiles to appointment selection and payment. I led the team and the UX research and design. Pratik Manjrekar contributed UI design; Nidhi S Kamath contributed UI and graphic design. Our final artifact was a prototype, not a clinical service.","reflection":"The survey raised a question we could not resolve in the challenge: how does someone stay anonymous while booking an accountable professional service? I would work through what is shared at each step before adding more features to the concept.","nextSteps":["Proposed validation: test whether participants understand what is private, what a provider can see, and whether the product offers booking or diagnosis.","Proposed iteration: add clear provider credentials, consent and cancellation information, then evaluate the booking journey with appropriate professional input."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 2–7","detail":"Named team roles, fourteen-hour constraint, survey size and reported responses."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 10–18","detail":"Persona, solution framing, user flow, wireframes and final booking screens."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p02.webp","caption":"The original team slide preserves who contributed what.","alt":"Team Elementors slide naming Srinidhi Bhat, Pratik Manjrekar and Nidhi S Kamath.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p17.webp","caption":"The designed route from discovering an expert to viewing details, choosing a date, and payment.","alt":"Original expert list, feed, expert profile, calendar and payment screens.","width":1600,"height":900}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/umatter.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":6,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"A clearer first step [[towards professional support.]]","scene":"Imagine being ready to explore professional support, but unsure who will see your details or what the first step involves. Every unclear commitment can make it easier to postpone the search.","question":"What should someone understand before being asked to book?","insight":"In our 65-response survey, almost half wanted professional help while remaining anonymous. [[Privacy expectations]] had to shape the journey before calendar and payment screens.","pivot":"Within a fourteen-hour challenge, I led the UX contribution toward a visible sequence: understand the provider, choose a time, then commit.","takeaway":"Trust begins with knowing what the next step asks of you.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Start with providers and support information."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"See the expert’s details before committing."},{"title":"Book","detail":"Move through calendar selection and payment."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the service boundary clear.","Earn confidence before asking for commitment.","Leave room for someone who is still exploring."],"annotations":[{"title":"Context before commitment","detail":"Expert details sit before the calendar and payment."},{"title":"A visible sequence","detail":"The concept separates discovery, consideration, and booking."},{"title":"A team outcome","detail":"I led UX research/design; Pratik and Nidhi contributed UI and graphic design."}],"evidenceTag":"Original research · 65 responses","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Original team research persona","label":"Shritija · student seeking support","context":"The original deck describes a medical student balancing studies with a need for professional support.","need":"Understand a provider and the first commitment before booking.","friction":"Limited time and a lack of support around seeking help.","implication":"Put provider information and privacy expectations before the appointment."}},"theme":{"layout":"care","mode":"light","paper":"#faf9ff","ink":"#242444","dim":"#62627c","line":"#dedced","accent":"#5356a8","wash":"#eeedfb","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Before asking for help","process":"Listening to the sample","decisions":"Designing for consideration","outcome":"The booking journey","reflection":"What trust needs next"},"decisionHeading":"Help people decide who to book.","resultHeading":"From considering support to choosing a time."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/umatter/p17.webp","alt":"Original expert list, feed, expert profile, calendar and payment screens.","caption":"The designed route from discovering an expert to viewing details, choosing a date, and payment.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf","width":1600,"height":900},"nextStory":{"slug":"mucoin","name":"MuCoin","headline":"Making the first budget less of a form."}},"mucoin":{"slug":"mucoin","name":"MuCoin","kind":"ux","headline":"Making the first budget less of a form.","summary":"A personal-finance onboarding concept that turns income, expenses, and savings goals into a reviewable starting picture.","role":"Product framing, onboarding flow & interface rationale","status":"Prototype & design rationale","context":"Personal finance · Progressive onboarding","facts":[{"value":"5","label":"onboarding stages"},{"value":"3","label":"review groups"},{"value":"Prototype","label":"linked in source"}],"problem":"A useful financial picture depends on personal information that takes effort to recall and can feel uncomfortable to share. I structured MuCoin around a sequence that asks for income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, savings goals, and a final review. The design challenge was to make that effort understandable and correctable.","process":["The original write-up explains the flow screen by screen, including privacy links, expense summaries, editable goals, and review behaviour. It also defines proposed KPIs for each stage. The design centres on repeatable entry, useful summaries and a review that remains editable.","The source mentions a small user subset when discussing the label Saving Goals, but gives no sample size, protocol, or measured comparison. I treat that as informal input. The numerical completion and drop-off figures in the appendix are targets, not results."],"decisions":[{"title":"Ask for a small, repeatable unit of information","choice":"The income stage starts with amount and source, while fixed and variable expenses use similar entry patterns.","reason":"A familiar pattern lets the person concentrate on their finances instead of relearning the interface at every step.","tradeoff":"A short form still needs room for irregular income, uncertain estimates, and multiple sources; simplicity cannot mean forcing inaccurate answers."},{"title":"Show a useful picture before onboarding ends","choice":"The expense stages return a chart, summary table, total, and relationship to income.","reason":"Summaries create opportunities to notice a missing entry or an implausible total while the information is still fresh.","tradeoff":"A chart is only as good as its inputs. It should communicate an estimate when the person is unsure, rather than imply financial precision."},{"title":"Make confirmation an editable checkpoint","choice":"The review groups income, expenses, and goals into three collapsible, editable sections.","reason":"Being able to inspect and correct information gives the person control over what they submit.","tradeoff":"The old rationale claims review ensures complete task success. That is a hypothesis to test; a confirmation screen cannot guarantee it."}],"outcome":"I designed a five-stage setup flow for income, expenses, savings goals and review. The entry patterns repeat, the summaries give something back along the way, and the final review stays editable. The accompanying KPI figures describe targets for a future test, not measured completion or drop-off.","reflection":"A useful setup should leave someone with a financial picture they understand. I would pay close attention to corrections: noticing and fixing an amount can be a better outcome than finishing the form quickly with the wrong numbers.","nextSteps":["Proposed measures: completion per stage, time to a usable summary, errors noticed during review, and understanding of what happens to entered information.","Proposed study: compare a short path with optional detail against the original sequence. Include irregular income and skipped estimates rather than testing only ideal inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"Original write-up, pp. 1–5","detail":"Project purpose and screen-by-screen design rationale."},{"label":"Original write-up, pp. 6–11","detail":"KPI definitions and aspirational targets; no measured results reported."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p02.webp","caption":"The source is a written design rationale. Its performance claims are reframed here as hypotheses.","alt":"Original MuCoin rationale for income and expense onboarding.","width":1120,"height":1585}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf","prototypeUrl":"https://www.figma.com/proto/fTSm9Chs0awMrjMUN2OWSa/MuCoin?node-id=1-2&starting-point-node-id=191%3A955&t=z97O9d2NsQAgujdk-1","cover":"assets/work/mucoin.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":9,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Making the first budget [[less of a form.]]","scene":"Picture someone setting up a finance app. They are recalling income, estimating expenses, and deciding how much personal information to share—all before seeing whether the product is useful.","question":"How can the setup give something back before it is finished?","insight":"The flow already contained a useful idea: [[show the emerging financial picture]] while a person enters it. Each summary can become a chance to understand and correct the information.","pivot":"I structured the onboarding as a repeatable sequence with visible progress, immediate summaries, and an editable checkpoint.","takeaway":"A good onboarding flow helps someone trust the information they just entered.","journey":[{"title":"Enter","detail":"Ask for a small, repeatable unit of information."},{"title":"See","detail":"Return a summary while the context is fresh."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Make the complete picture editable before submission."}],"decisionLeads":["Start with one manageable question.","Give the input a visible consequence.","Let review become an act of control."],"annotations":[{"title":"Five connected stages","detail":"Income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, goals, and review."},{"title":"A measurement plan","detail":"The original percentages are targets for evaluation, not achieved results."}],"evidenceTag":"Original flow rationale · five stages","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person setting up a budget","context":"Preparing a first picture of income, spending, and savings.","need":"Enter information accurately and understand what it adds up to.","friction":"Recall takes effort; mistakes are easy to make before the full picture is visible.","implication":"Give input an immediate summary and an editable review."}},"theme":{"layout":"ledger","mode":"light","paper":"#f4f8ff","ink":"#142d57","dim":"#536782","line":"#d0dded","accent":"#205bb7","wash":"#e1edff","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"The cost of setup","process":"A picture worth reviewing","decisions":"Five stages, one pattern","outcome":"The proposed onboarding","reflection":"Measuring understanding"},"decisionHeading":"Make each onboarding step useful.","resultHeading":"A financial picture that can be corrected."},"visual":{"presentation":"document","src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p02.webp","alt":"Original MuCoin rationale for income and expense onboarding.","caption":"The source is a written design rationale. Its performance claims are reframed here as hypotheses.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf","width":1120,"height":1585},"nextStory":{"slug":"gmd","name":"GetMetaData","headline":"An SEO panel you can actually scan."}},"gmd":{"slug":"gmd","name":"GetMetaData","kind":"ux","headline":"An SEO panel you can actually scan.","summary":"A browser-extension redesign exploring information hierarchy for people with different levels of SEO experience.","role":"Competitive review, information architecture, wireframes & UI","status":"Redesign prototype","context":"Chrome extension · SEO inspection","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"competitors reviewed"},{"value":"1","label":"shared comparison site"},{"value":"2","label":"persona perspectives"}],"problem":"GetMetaData exposes a page’s SEO elements, but a dense readout makes it difficult to see what needs attention. The original review describes small text, weak information architecture, and a repeated-output bug. My task was to improve inspection without stripping away the useful detail or export capability.","process":["I compared SEO META in 1 CLICK, All in One SEO Analyzer, and Detailed SEO Extension alongside GetMetaData. I used my own portfolio as the common test page and looked at functionality, usability, SEO information, and documentation. This kept the content context consistent, although one website could not represent every page structure.","I then used two persona perspectives—an SEO engineer and a small-business/content-marketing user—to frame different reading needs, before creating wireframes and a high-fidelity prototype. The deck does not document interviews validating those personas, so I use them as design lenses rather than research findings."],"decisions":[{"title":"Retain information depth, improve the route through it","choice":"The redesign explores a clearer hierarchy for metadata, headings, images, structured data, and links.","reason":"An inspector should help a person locate relevant evidence before asking them to interpret a full page of metrics.","tradeoff":"A more segmented interface can make cross-category comparison harder. Testing should include jumping between related issues."},{"title":"Compare tools in a controlled content context","choice":"I reviewed the competing extensions against the same portfolio page.","reason":"Using one common page makes differences in presentation easier to discuss without changing the underlying website at every comparison.","tradeoff":"The review is a historical snapshot on one site, not a current market ranking or a large performance benchmark."},{"title":"Separate interface redesign from a functional repair","choice":"The review records duplicated output as a bug, while the deliverable remains an interface prototype.","reason":"Making data easier to read would not fix incorrect or repeated extraction. The two problems need separate acceptance criteria.","tradeoff":"The prototype can demonstrate hierarchy and navigation; it cannot prove the extraction bug was resolved."}],"outcome":"I completed the competitor review, wireframes and high-fidelity prototype. The redesign groups the extension’s information into clearer destinations while retaining its depth and export workflow. I recorded the repeated-output bug separately; an interface prototype could not establish whether extraction had been fixed.","reflection":"The next test should use real inspection tasks: find a missing description, check headings, compare related issues and export the findings. That would tell me more than asking whether the new panel looks cleaner.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a novice and an experienced practitioner to locate a missing title, inspect image descriptions, and export their findings.","Proposed acceptance criteria: correct extraction without duplication, clear reading order, and useful handling of a page with unusually long or missing values."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 3–9","detail":"Problem, comparison method, historical tool observations and comparison mapping."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 10–14","detail":"Persona perspectives, wireframes and final prototype."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/p08.webp","caption":"The original review separates useful capabilities from readability and data-quality problems.","alt":"Original GetMetaData strengths and weaknesses slide.","width":1600,"height":900},{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/p14.webp","caption":"The final prototype provides the visual outcome; task-based validation remains the next step.","alt":"Original high-fidelity GetMetaData extension design.","width":1600,"height":900}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf","prototypeUrl":"https://www.figma.com/proto/P86VsJJUoQDMzxsUFCnRHo/3MMaven-Project?node-id=8-2&starting-point-node-id=8%3A2&t=K4zjIkNOs7p8WSiR-1","cover":"assets/work/gmd.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":12,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"An SEO panel [[you can actually scan.]]","scene":"Imagine opening an SEO extension to answer one question about a page. The tool has plenty of data, but finding the relevant item becomes another inspection task.","question":"What should stay visible when an inspector becomes easier to read?","insight":"The historical review found [[useful depth and export]] alongside small text, weak organisation, and duplicated output. A visual refresh needed to preserve the valuable workflow.","pivot":"I separated three concerns: finding information, interpreting it, and making sure the extraction itself is correct.","takeaway":"Information depth becomes useful when there is a clear route through it.","journey":[{"title":"Locate","detail":"Get to the relevant category."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Read the page evidence in context."},{"title":"Carry forward","detail":"Keep export useful in the wider workflow."}],"decisionLeads":["Give the data a reading order.","Hold the comparison context steady.","Keep correctness on its own checklist."],"annotations":[{"title":"Grouped information","detail":"The high-fidelity prototype explores a clearer inspection hierarchy."},{"title":"A usable work surface","detail":"Readability and navigation are part of the tool’s utility."},{"title":"A separate acceptance criterion","detail":"The repeated-output bug needs an product behaviour fix beyond this prototype."}],"evidenceTag":"Historical review · three competitors","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Original persona framing · not interview-validated","label":"SEO specialist & small-business content owner","context":"Two personas in the original deck frame expert inspection and a less technical reading need.","need":"Find and understand relevant page information.","friction":"Dense lists make scanning difficult; unfamiliar terms make the same display harder for a novice.","implication":"Preserve depth while creating clearer groups and entry points."}},"theme":{"layout":"inspector","mode":"light","paper":"#f6faff","ink":"#203146","dim":"#516984","line":"#cbdbee","accent":"#286cad","wash":"#e4effc","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A dense readout","process":"Two ways of reading","decisions":"Making inspection clearer","outcome":"The redesigned extension","reflection":"Testing the hierarchy"},"decisionHeading":"Help different users scan the same metadata.","resultHeading":"A more structured inspection surface."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/gmd/p14.webp","alt":"Original high-fidelity GetMetaData extension design.","caption":"The final prototype provides the visual outcome; task-based validation remains the next step.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf","width":1600,"height":900},"nextStory":{"slug":"steve-wilson","name":"Steve Wilson","headline":"Let the work lead the portfolio."}},"steve-wilson":{"slug":"steve-wilson","name":"Steve Wilson","kind":"ux","headline":"Let the work lead the portfolio.","summary":"An early portfolio concept exploring typography, project browsing, and a repeatable case-study template.","role":"Visual direction, page structure & interface concept","status":"Independent visual concept","context":"Designer portfolio · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"page types"},{"value":"Type-led","label":"visual direction"},{"value":"Concept","label":"not a client engagement"}],"problem":"A portfolio has to communicate what a designer does, offer a convincing sample of their work, and make contact straightforward. This early concept uses the fictional Steve Wilson identity to explore that sequence through a home page, work listing, project-detail template, and contact destination.","process":["The available evidence is a four-page visual artifact, including a long project-detail template. It contains sample copy, mock project names, placeholder results, and a sample client attribution. Those are layout content, not my client history or achieved results.","I organised the pages so that the introduction establishes the designer’s role, the work listing exposes project categories and tools, and the detail template reserves space for the problem, process, outcome, and lessons. No original research or testing record accompanies it."],"decisions":[{"title":"Use typography to establish the first impression","choice":"The concept combines a large name treatment with a restrained dark background and a bright accent.","reason":"The visual hierarchy gives the identity a clear point of entry before visitors scan services or projects.","tradeoff":"A strong name treatment cannot replace useful positioning copy; I would make the designer’s particular value more specific."},{"title":"Separate browsing from deep reading","choice":"The work listing acts as an index, while project details have a longer narrative structure.","reason":"Visitors can skim the portfolio before deciding which story deserves more attention.","tradeoff":"A listing only helps if its labels explain the work rather than relying on unfamiliar project names."},{"title":"Give each project the same narrative spine","choice":"The template includes role, problem, process, results, takeaways, and final product.","reason":"A repeated structure helps a reviewer compare contributions across projects.","tradeoff":"In the archive, much of that structure is empty sample content. A template is useful scaffolding, not evidence of a completed case study."}],"outcome":"I designed a homepage, work index, project-detail template and contact page around the fictional Steve Wilson identity. The exercise helped me explore the difference between scanning a body of work and reading one project closely. Names, client credits and result figures in the original template are sample content.","reflection":"I would keep the clear browsing structure and rewrite the project pages around actual decisions. A well-proportioned template is useful, but the writing and evidence inside it are what make a portfolio convincing.","nextSteps":["Proposed iteration: replace the sample project with one real story, then test whether a reviewer can identify the role, central decision, and outcome in a short scan.","Proposed UI review: verify contrast, keyboard navigation, responsive reading order, and a concise contact path before implementation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, pp. 1–4","detail":"Home, work list, project-detail template and contact; sample identities and results are concept content."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p01.webp","caption":"Original home-page concept: identity, introduction, and services.","alt":"Dark Steve Wilson home-page concept with a yellow accent.","width":1600,"height":1800},{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p02.webp","caption":"The work list keeps project browsing separate from the longer case-study template.","alt":"Original Steve Wilson project listing.","width":1600,"height":1800}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/swportfolio.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":15,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Let the work [[lead the portfolio.]]","scene":"Picture a reviewer opening a designer’s portfolio between other applications. The first page has to establish what the designer does and make one relevant project easy to choose.","question":"What makes someone want to move from a first impression into the work?","insight":"The original concept already separates [[browsing from deep reading]]. Its project template creates room for a story, but that room still needs real decisions and evidence.","pivot":"I used the retrospective to connect the visual hierarchy to the reviewer’s sequence: orient, select, investigate, then make contact.","takeaway":"The layout earns attention; the evidence earns confidence.","journey":[{"title":"Orient","detail":"Understand the designer’s role."},{"title":"Choose","detail":"Scan a focused work index."},{"title":"Investigate","detail":"Open a project with room for contribution and process."}],"decisionLeads":["Establish a clear first impression.","Let browsing remain lightweight.","Give the detail page something real to say."],"annotations":[{"title":"A type-led opening","detail":"The name treatment establishes the visual entry point."},{"title":"A separate work index","detail":"Projects can be scanned before opening the longer story."}],"evidenceTag":"Original visual concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Prospective client reviewing a portfolio","context":"Scanning for relevant work before deciding whether to make contact.","need":"Understand the designer’s offer and inspect a suitable example.","friction":"A polished introduction alone does not explain the work.","implication":"Move quickly from identity to selected projects."}},"theme":{"layout":"portfolio","mode":"dark","paper":"#101010","ink":"#f5f4ed","dim":"#c3c3b8","line":"#3f3f37","accent":"#f1d548","wash":"#343019","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A first impression","process":"The useful signal","decisions":"Identity into evidence","outcome":"The portfolio concept","reflection":"Adding project depth"},"decisionHeading":"Lead from the work into the case study.","resultHeading":"A strong front door, with depth still to add."},"visual":{"presentation":"split","src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p02.webp","alt":"Original Steve Wilson project listing.","caption":"The work list keeps project browsing separate from the longer case-study template.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf","width":1600,"height":1800},"nextStory":{"slug":"gloria-furniture","name":"Gloria Furniture","headline":"A furniture shop organised around the room."}},"gloria-furniture":{"slug":"gloria-furniture","name":"Gloria Furniture","kind":"ux","headline":"A furniture shop organised around the room.","summary":"A furniture storefront concept connecting room-led browsing, product comparison, and purchase actions.","role":"Visual design & storefront information hierarchy","status":"Storefront concept","context":"E-commerce · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"featured product groups"},{"value":"Category-led","label":"browsing"},{"value":"Concept","label":"no sales results recorded"}],"problem":"Furniture is both a visual and a practical purchase. The original Gloria concept tries to support inspiration at the top of the page, category exploration in the middle, and concrete products with prices further down. The design question is how to make those modes feel like one useful shopping path.","process":["The surviving PDF is a single long storefront design, not a research report. I reviewed its hero, category imagery, quality messaging, product cards, and service links to make the underlying structure explicit. The archived dollar prices, warranty copy, and contact details are sample storefront content, not verified business facts.","This is a retrospective reading of the design. The available artifact does not establish customer interviews, a checkout implementation, or commercial performance."],"decisions":[{"title":"Start with inspiration, then offer more specific choices","choice":"The hero leads into visual categories before the featured Home Decor and Furniture groups.","reason":"A shopper can begin with a broad interest and progressively narrow to a product, instead of meeting a full catalogue immediately.","tradeoff":"The path would need descriptive category labels and accessible images; atmosphere alone does not tell a shopper where to go."},{"title":"Make product cards comparable","choice":"The featured groups repeat name, price, image, Add to Cart, and Buy Now treatments.","reason":"A stable card structure supports scanning across products and makes purchase actions predictable.","tradeoff":"Two equally prominent purchase actions can compete. Today I would test a primary product-detail path before offering immediate purchase everywhere."},{"title":"Keep practical reassurance near the shopping journey","choice":"The concept includes a quality band and footer links for stores, warranty claims, and custom requests.","reason":"Furniture decisions often require service and fulfilment information as well as appealing photography.","tradeoff":"Those claims need real policies and product information. The visual concept alone cannot establish quality or a working support service."}],"outcome":"I designed a storefront landing page that moves from room inspiration into categories and comparable product cards. Prices and service information sit alongside the imagery so browsing has a practical direction. The artifact is a visual concept; it does not include a working checkout.","reflection":"I would next add the details someone needs before buying furniture: dimensions, materials, stock and delivery coverage. Those are more useful at this stage than another promotional section.","nextSteps":["Proposed task: find a product within a budget, compare two options, and explain delivery expectations. Record missing information as well as completion.","Proposed iteration: build a product-detail page and a narrow-screen catalogue, using real product data and one clearly prioritised action."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Full storefront: hero, category browsing, quality messages, featured groups and service footer."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_01.webp","caption":"The original hierarchy moves from an aspirational hero to category-led browsing.","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture hero and category layout.","width":1400,"height":1969},{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_02.webp","caption":"Repeated product cards carry the browsing pattern through to purchase actions.","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture featured product groups.","width":1400,"height":1969}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/gloriafurniture.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":18,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"A furniture shop [[organised around the room.]]","scene":"Imagine finding a piece of furniture you like, then asking whether it fits the room, the budget, and the practicalities of buying it. A storefront has to support both kinds of attention.","question":"How does visual browsing become a useful buying decision?","insight":"The concept moves from [[inspiration to categories to comparable products]]. That sequence is the useful foundation; dimensions and fulfilment would make the next step stronger.","pivot":"I organised the retrospective around how the page narrows a shopper’s options, then identified the information still needed to support a purchase.","takeaway":"A beautiful product card should help someone decide what to do next.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Start from an image or a category."},{"title":"Compare","detail":"Use a repeated card pattern to scan options."},{"title":"Decide","detail":"Bring practical product and delivery information into the next step."}],"decisionLeads":["Give inspiration a direction.","Make comparison predictable.","Connect reassurance to real information."],"annotations":[{"title":"Category-led discovery","detail":"The upper page offers ways into the catalogue."},{"title":"Repeated product cards","detail":"The lower page carries names, prices, and actions in a consistent pattern."}],"evidenceTag":"Original storefront concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Furniture shopper making a shortlist","context":"Browsing room inspiration and comparing categories.","need":"Find a relevant collection and enough detail to continue.","friction":"A visual catalogue can leave practical questions unanswered.","implication":"Connect inspiration to categories and a next product decision."}},"theme":{"layout":"catalogue","mode":"light","paper":"#fffaf8","ink":"#2b2320","dim":"#79615a","line":"#e8d9d1","accent":"#bc273a","wash":"#f9e2e2","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A room in mind","process":"Inspiration needs a route","decisions":"From room to product","outcome":"The storefront concept","reflection":"Beyond the first screen"},"decisionHeading":"Help shoppers compare a collection.","resultHeading":"A catalogue organised around the room."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_02.webp","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture featured product groups.","caption":"Repeated product cards carry the browsing pattern through to purchase actions.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf","width":1400,"height":1969},"nextStory":{"slug":"fun-cruises","name":"Fun Cruises Goa","headline":"Choosing a boat before making an enquiry."}},"fun-cruises":{"slug":"fun-cruises","name":"Fun Cruises Goa","kind":"ux","headline":"Choosing a boat before making an enquiry.","summary":"A yacht-rental landing-page concept organised around capacity, price, and the next enquiry.","role":"Landing-page structure, fleet browsing & visual design","status":"Website redesign concept","context":"Travel & leisure · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"fleet filter dimensions"},{"value":"Capacity","label":"visible on listings"},{"value":"Enquiry","label":"core next step"}],"problem":"A visitor choosing a boat needs to know whether it fits their group and budget before making an enquiry. The Fun Cruises Goa artifact contains the building blocks for that decision: a prominent booking action, a description of the experience, fleet filters, and listings with seating capacity and starting hourly prices.","process":["I reviewed the original long-page design as a visitor journey from initial interest to a suitable fleet option. The available PDF shows the landing and fleet-discovery surface. It does not include completed reservation, payment, or availability states, so I describe the scope as discovery and enquiry.","The rates, capacities, address, and response-time copy visible in the old concept are historical design content. This case study uses their placement to explain hierarchy, rather than presenting them as current offers."],"decisions":[{"title":"Bring decision criteria into the fleet list","choice":"The design offers seating, price-order, and boat-type controls, with capacity and starting rate on individual listings.","reason":"A visitor should be able to rule out unsuitable options without opening every boat’s detail page.","tradeoff":"Filters depend on consistent inventory data. Empty results and combinations that return no suitable boat would need explicit handling."},{"title":"Support both ready-to-book and exploratory visitors","choice":"The concept has a hero booking action and an enquiry route alongside fleet details.","reason":"Some visitors know what they want; others need clarification before committing. The page makes both next steps available.","tradeoff":"Booking, getting a quote, and learning more can become competing calls to action. I would clarify which action each listing should lead to."},{"title":"Use imagery with practical content","choice":"Fleet photographs sit beside capacity, starting price, and short descriptions.","reason":"The images convey the experience while the text helps establish whether the option is suitable.","tradeoff":"A starting hourly price does not communicate the full cost. Minimum duration, inclusions, fees, and availability would need to be explained next."}],"outcome":"I redesigned the landing and fleet-browsing page around boat type, capacity and starting rate. The imagery introduces the experience, while the listings give visitors something concrete to compare before enquiring. Availability and a completed reservation flow are outside the delivered concept.","reflection":"An enquiry should not look like a confirmed booking. I would make that distinction explicit, then add the inclusions, accessibility details and cancellation terms needed to discuss a trip. Prices in the original design are historical concept content.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a visitor planning for a group to choose a suitable option, explain the expected cost, and identify what remains to confirm.","Proposed extension: add a boat-detail page with availability, inclusions, accessibility information, cancellation terms, and a clear enquiry handoff."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Hero, quote action, seating/price/type filters, fleet cards and contact footer."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_01.webp","caption":"The upper page connects the experience to a visible booking action and fleet filters.","alt":"Original Fun Cruises Goa hero, introduction and fleet filters.","width":1400,"height":2166},{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_02.webp","caption":"Fleet listings surface practical information next to the experience imagery.","alt":"Original Fun Cruises boat listings with capacity and price content.","width":1400,"height":2166}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/funcruises.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":21,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Choosing a boat [[before making an enquiry.]]","scene":"Picture planning an outing with a group. Before an enquiry, the practical questions arrive: how many people fit, what does it cost, and which type of boat makes sense?","question":"Which questions can the fleet list answer before a visitor opens every option?","insight":"The strongest part of the concept is its [[comparable fleet information]]: seating, starting rate, and boat type accompany the photographs.","pivot":"I treated the landing page as a route into a suitable enquiry, with discovery criteria that narrow the choices.","takeaway":"Good discovery makes the next conversation more informed.","journey":[{"title":"Narrow","detail":"Use capacity, price, and type to find suitable options."},{"title":"Compare","detail":"Read practical information alongside the experience."},{"title":"Enquire","detail":"Confirm availability, inclusions, and the complete cost."}],"decisionLeads":["Answer the comparison questions early.","Support different levels of booking readiness.","Explain what the starting rate leaves to confirm."],"annotations":[{"title":"Decision criteria near the fleet","detail":"Filters appear before the visitor scans individual boats."},{"title":"Imagery with practical content","detail":"Listings pair the experience with capacity and price information."}],"evidenceTag":"Original fleet-discovery concept","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Group organiser considering a boat","context":"Comparing options for a group occasion in Goa.","need":"Identify a suitable boat before making an enquiry.","friction":"Capacity, starting price, and suitability must be read together.","implication":"Keep the practical comparison close to each listing."}},"theme":{"layout":"voyage","mode":"light","paper":"#fffcf6","ink":"#203c40","dim":"#5a7174","line":"#d6ded7","accent":"#866117","wash":"#faedcb","bodyFont":"Lato","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Planning for a group","process":"The practical shortlist","decisions":"Designing the enquiry","outcome":"The fleet experience","reflection":"Questions before booking"},"decisionHeading":"Help a group compare the boats.","resultHeading":"A clearer route to a suitable enquiry."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_02.webp","alt":"Original Fun Cruises boat listings with capacity and price content.","caption":"Fleet listings surface practical information next to the experience imagery.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf","width":1400,"height":2166},"nextStory":{"slug":"bni-website","name":"BNI Goa","headline":"Meet the chapter. Know how to take part."}},"bni-website":{"slug":"bni-website","name":"BNI Goa","kind":"ux","headline":"Meet the chapter. Know how to take part.","summary":"A chapter-website concept that connects the organisation’s purpose, the people behind it, and a first visit.","role":"Information hierarchy & website visual design","status":"Chapter website concept","context":"BNI Victory Goa · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"People-led","label":"credibility"},{"value":"Visit","label":"primary invitation"},{"value":"Concept","label":"sample member content"}],"problem":"A person considering a business network needs to understand its purpose, who participates, and how to take a first step. The BNI Victory Goa design brings those questions into a local chapter website with an invitation, organisation overview, leadership section, member directory, and contact form.","process":["The available PDF is a long-page visual concept. I reviewed the sequence from the hero invitation through purpose and people to contact. The artifact does not contain an interview report, launch evidence, or a membership funnel.","The member cards repeat placeholder identities and informal sample biographies. The overview also contains network-wide statistics. Neither is evidence of chapter growth or my project impact, so those details are not promoted as results here."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give the visitor a concrete first step","choice":"The hero uses a Get Invited action instead of making the organisation overview the only starting point.","reason":"An invitation turns interest in networking into an understandable next action.","tradeoff":"The next screen would need to explain meeting expectations and eligibility; a button label alone does not establish that service."},{"title":"Make the organisation visible through its people","choice":"Leadership and member-card patterns occupy distinct sections.","reason":"The directory can help a visitor understand the kinds of businesses represented and who is responsible for the chapter.","tradeoff":"Repeated mock content weakens credibility. Production would require member permission, verified biographies, and a reliable update process."},{"title":"Keep purpose and contact in the same journey","choice":"The page connects the chapter overview and mission to a form and local contact details.","reason":"A visitor can move from understanding the organisation to asking a practical question without searching elsewhere.","tradeoff":"The long single-page structure would benefit from a tighter summary and explicit links to meeting details."}],"outcome":"I designed a chapter-site concept that brings the organisation’s purpose, leadership, members and invitation into one sequence. Reusable member patterns give the page a structure that could grow with the chapter. The archived design contains sample biographies and is not a record of actual member results.","reflection":"Before expanding the directory, I would confirm the local chapter information, member permissions and visitor process. The people and meeting details need to be accurate for the invitation to be useful.","nextSteps":["Proposed iteration: replace all sample biographies with verified chapter content and explain the visitor-meeting process before the invitation action.","Proposed evaluation: ask a prospective visitor what the chapter does, whether it is relevant to their business, and what will happen after they request an invitation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Invitation, organisation overview, mission, leader/member layouts and contact; repeated cards are placeholders."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/p01_01.webp","caption":"The original chapter introduction connects the invitation to a purpose-led overview.","alt":"Original BNI Victory Goa hero and organisation overview.","width":1400,"height":2411}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/bniwebsite.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":24,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"Meet the chapter. [[Know how to take part.]]","scene":"Imagine considering a business network for the first time. You want to understand who participates, why they meet, and whether you could take a first look without already knowing someone.","question":"What turns an organisation overview into an approachable first visit?","insight":"The chapter concept gives the visitor [[a purpose, people, and an invitation]]. Those pieces need accurate local content to become credible.","pivot":"I connected the page structure to a prospective member’s sequence of questions, keeping the invitation visible alongside the chapter story.","takeaway":"Community credibility comes from real people and a clear next step.","journey":[{"title":"Understand","detail":"Learn what the chapter is for."},{"title":"Recognise","detail":"Meet the people and kinds of businesses represented."},{"title":"Visit","detail":"Find out how an invitation works."}],"decisionLeads":["Make a first visit imaginable.","Give responsibility a human face.","Let useful local information carry the invitation."],"annotations":[{"title":"A concrete invitation","detail":"The hero offers a first step into the organisation."},{"title":"A purpose-led introduction","detail":"The overview and mission explain why the chapter exists."}],"evidenceTag":"Original chapter-site concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Business owner exploring a local chapter","context":"Considering whether the network is relevant and how to participate.","need":"Understand local people, purpose, and the next step.","friction":"Global organisation information does not answer local membership questions.","implication":"Bring the chapter’s people and participation path forward."}},"theme":{"layout":"chapter","mode":"light","paper":"#fffefe","ink":"#32262a","dim":"#76636a","line":"#ead6dc","accent":"#b7213c","wash":"#fae5e9","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A local membership question","process":"What a chapter must explain","decisions":"Designing participation","outcome":"The chapter concept","reflection":"Content before scale"},"decisionHeading":"Connect the chapter’s purpose to joining.","resultHeading":"A membership story with a next step."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/bni-website/p01_01.webp","alt":"Original BNI Victory Goa hero and organisation overview.","caption":"The original chapter introduction connects the invitation to a purpose-led overview.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf","width":1400,"height":2411},"nextStory":{"slug":"irctc","name":"IRCTC","headline":"The train search comes first."}},"irctc":{"slug":"irctc","name":"IRCTC","kind":"ux","headline":"The train search comes first.","summary":"An independent homepage concept exploring the hierarchy between a primary booking task and a large set of secondary services.","role":"Task hierarchy & interface redesign concept","status":"Independent redesign concept","context":"Travel booking · No organisational affiliation","facts":[{"value":"Search-first","label":"page hierarchy"},{"value":"5","label":"core journey inputs"},{"value":"Concept","label":"homepage scope"}],"problem":"A railway homepage serves many audiences, but searching for a train is a clear primary task. My independent IRCTC concept places the booking form prominently while retaining entry points for PNR status, vacancies, concessions, and related travel services. The challenge is hierarchy: helping the common task stay visible without erasing important exceptions.","process":["The source is one full homepage design, not a documented end-to-end booking redesign. I evaluated the visible grouping of From, To, Date, Class, and Quota, the surrounding service links, and the separation of holiday content below the search area.","There is no original usability study or live implementation in the provided material. Dates, phone numbers, and offers in the mockup are sample or historical interface content, not current travel guidance."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the core journey fields together","choice":"The concept groups origin, destination, travel date, class, and quota in a single booking panel with a visible Search action.","reason":"The person can form a complete query in one recognisable task area.","tradeoff":"The retained panel still contains many specialised options. Today I would test how to reveal less common settings without hiding essential access needs."},{"title":"Distinguish a current journey from trip inspiration","choice":"Related services and curated holidays appear beneath the primary search region.","reason":"A person trying to book a known route should not have to read promotional content first.","tradeoff":"The holiday cards remain text-heavy. Visual separation helps hierarchy but does not by itself solve page density."},{"title":"Retain explicit entry points for adjacent rail tasks","choice":"PNR status and chart/vacancy shortcuts remain accessible near the booking area.","reason":"People returning to manage an existing journey have a different task from people searching for a new one.","tradeoff":"The homepage only establishes entry points. It does not demonstrate those destination flows or their error handling."}],"outcome":"I created an independent homepage concept with a clear train-search area and grouped entry points for secondary rail services. The work concentrates on the starting task. Search results, changing availability and the rest of the booking flow were not part of this concept.","reflection":"I would take the same hierarchy into a difficult journey: no seats available, a changed fare or an unsuitable route. A quieter homepage only helps if the following screens make those decisions understandable too.","nextSteps":["Proposed test: find a train, check an existing journey, and locate a concession option; compare discovery and mistakes without assuming that a shorter visual scan means a successful booking.","Proposed extension: add no-results, changing availability, route correction, keyboard navigation, and narrow-screen form states."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Independent homepage mockup showing journey fields, rail-task shortcuts, related services and holidays."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/p01_01.webp","caption":"The concept makes the booking form the visual centre while retaining adjacent rail tasks.","alt":"Original independent IRCTC redesign with booking panel and service shortcuts.","width":1400,"height":1969}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/irctc.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":27,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"The train search [[comes first.]]","scene":"Picture arriving at a railway homepage with a route and a date in mind. Other services matter, but the immediate task is to form a complete train search.","question":"How should a dense service homepage protect its primary task?","insight":"The concept gives the search [[one recognisable task area]], while retaining adjacent rail services and placing trip inspiration further down.","pivot":"I focused the redesign on hierarchy: group the journey inputs, preserve useful shortcuts, and make the difference between booking and browsing legible.","takeaway":"Hierarchy should make the common task clear without erasing important exceptions.","journey":[{"title":"Form the query","detail":"Keep route, date, class, and quota together."},{"title":"Search","detail":"Give the completed query one clear action."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Carry the task into results and recovery states next."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the primary inputs together.","Let inspiration follow the immediate task.","Preserve a route for people managing an existing journey."],"annotations":[{"title":"A central task area","detail":"The booking panel groups the core journey inputs."},{"title":"Adjacent rail tasks","detail":"PNR and vacancy entry points remain nearby."}],"evidenceTag":"Independent homepage concept","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Traveller starting a rail search","context":"Arriving with a route and a possible date in mind.","need":"Start a train search without losing the primary task.","friction":"Many services and promotions compete for attention.","implication":"Give journey inputs first priority."}},"theme":{"layout":"departure","mode":"light","paper":"#f8faff","ink":"#162c50","dim":"#536785","line":"#d0dced","accent":"#253a96","wash":"#e5eafa","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A journey to begin","process":"The task worth protecting","decisions":"Clearing the search path","outcome":"The homepage concept","reflection":"Testing the first search"},"decisionHeading":"Keep train booking at the centre.","resultHeading":"Search leads. Other services follow."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/irctc/p01_01.webp","alt":"Original independent IRCTC redesign with booking panel and service shortcuts.","caption":"The concept makes the booking form the visual centre while retaining adjacent rail tasks.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf","width":1400,"height":1969},"nextStory":{"slug":"nityananda","name":"Nitya Nanda","headline":"A photography site that leaves room for the photographs."}},"nityananda":{"slug":"nityananda","name":"Nitya Nanda","kind":"ux","headline":"A photography site that leaves room for the photographs.","summary":"A photography portfolio concept with a deliberately restrained interface and two clear ways into the work.","role":"Portfolio structure & visual design","status":"Independent portfolio concept","context":"Photography · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"work categories"},{"value":"3","label":"primary destinations"},{"value":"Image-led","label":"visual hierarchy"}],"problem":"A photography portfolio needs to show the work before asking visitors to read a long introduction. The Nitya Nanda concept explores a restrained structure with About, Work, and Contact destinations, an image-led opening, and separate professional and freelance collections.","process":["The available source is a single long visual concept. I traced how the page introduces the photographer, groups the work, and leads to the contact form. The listed shoots, locations, and credentials are illustrative portfolio content; the artifact does not substantiate them as real commissions.","This retrospective draws design observations from the page itself. No original audience research, client engagement record, or booking measurements accompany the concept."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the navigation vocabulary small","choice":"The page repeats About, Work, and Contact around an image-led presentation.","reason":"These are familiar questions for someone considering a creative professional and make the portfolio easy to orient within.","tradeoff":"Repeated navigation should help orientation rather than consume space. Today I would test a single persistent version on smaller screens."},{"title":"Separate two ways of reading the portfolio","choice":"Professional and freelance work have their own grouped image collections with captions.","reason":"The distinction gives visitors a route based on the kind of commission they are considering.","tradeoff":"The grouping is a design assumption. Real enquiries might be better served by subject, service, or photographic style instead."},{"title":"Give the visual journey a practical endpoint","choice":"A short contact form follows the portfolio, with additional work and consultation links.","reason":"Someone convinced by the images should have a direct route to starting a conversation.","tradeoff":"The concept stops at submission. A complete service needs clear expectations, error messages, and confirmation of what happens next."}],"outcome":"I designed a restrained portfolio with professional and freelance collections, a small navigation system and a contact destination. The layout lets visitors browse images before deciding to make an enquiry. The biography and photography credentials in the concept are illustrative.","reflection":"I would spend the next pass on selecting images, writing useful captions and checking loading performance. The interface has already done enough when it helps someone understand the work and find a way to get in touch.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask visitors choosing a photographer to identify a relevant body of work and start an enquiry; observe which category language they expect.","Proposed implementation: responsive image sizes, descriptive alternatives, keyboard access, and a complete form confirmation and error flow."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Photographer introduction, professional/freelance galleries, contact form and illustrative project captions."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/p01.webp","caption":"The original full-page concept: images carry the story and contact closes the sequence.","alt":"Original Nitya Nanda photography portfolio layout.","width":1400,"height":3557}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/nityananda.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":30,"pdfVersion":"6be7b739ef08","narrative":{"headline":"A photography site [[that leaves room for the photographs.]]","scene":"Imagine choosing a photographer. You want to see relevant work, get a feel for the person behind it, and know how to begin an enquiry.","question":"How much interface does an image-led portfolio actually need?","insight":"The concept uses [[three familiar destinations]] and two work groupings to keep the route through the photography understandable.","pivot":"I treated restraint as a structural choice: let the images carry the first impression, then give the visitor a practical endpoint.","takeaway":"A quiet interface still needs to give clear directions.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Let images establish the first impression."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Choose a relevant collection."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Start an enquiry with clear expectations."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep orientation familiar.","Group the work around a useful distinction.","Give the visual journey a practical endpoint."],"annotations":[{"title":"Images carry the story","detail":"The concept keeps the interface around a large body of work."},{"title":"Contact closes the sequence","detail":"The enquiry form gives interest somewhere to go."}],"evidenceTag":"Original photography-portfolio concept","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person considering a photographer","context":"Looking for a relevant style and a way to discuss a shoot.","need":"See a coherent body of work and begin an enquiry.","friction":"A broad gallery can make relevance hard to judge.","implication":"Use collections and a clear contact route."}},"theme":{"layout":"gallery","mode":"dark","paper":"#101010","ink":"#f2eee8","dim":"#bcb5ac","line":"#3d3832","accent":"#d9c6a9","wash":"#342e26","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Cormorant Garamond","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Looking for a style","process":"Letting the work speak","decisions":"A portfolio with space","outcome":"The photography concept","reflection":"Making relevance clearer"},"decisionHeading":"Let visitors browse the photographs.","resultHeading":"A body of work before a sales pitch."},"visual":{"presentation":"split","src":"assets/cases/nityananda/p01.webp","alt":"Original Nitya Nanda photography portfolio layout.","caption":"The original full-page concept: images carry the story and contact closes the sequence.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf","width":1400,"height":3557},"nextStory":{"slug":"arkitype","name":"Arkitype","headline":"Change a token. See what it changes."}},"arkitype":{"slug":"arkitype","name":"Arkitype","kind":"product","headline":"Change a token. See what it changes.","summary":"A working design-system studio that links foundations, component behaviour, previews, and exports through one model.","role":"Product idea, design-system UX & interaction direction","status":"Public beta","context":"Design systems · Working product","facts":[{"value":"90","label":"components in the system registry"},{"value":"8","label":"decision areas"},{"value":"Shared","label":"token-to-export model"}],"problem":"A palette, a component library, and a handoff document can each look convincing while disagreeing with one another. I approached Arkitype as a connected workspace: decisions about colour, type, space, shape, and motion should remain visible in the components and artifacts they produce.","process":["I framed the workspace around eight connected design areas, rather than a compulsory wizard. A designer should be able to revisit a foundation while considering a component and still understand what depends on that decision.","The UX goal is continuity: foundations, component states, previews, and handoff should feel like parts of one system. The current 90-component library is product scope; it does not establish that a new user understands the workspace."],"decisions":[{"title":"Use dependencies to guide, rather than enforce a sequence","choice":"Foundations, assets, components, and preview remain accessible, with relationships expressed as Built from and Lands in links.","reason":"The navigation reflects iterative design work instead of pretending every system starts and ends in the same order.","tradeoff":"An open workspace gives less automatic direction. First-use guidance must help a person find a useful starting point."},{"title":"Keep design changes consistent across surfaces","choice":"The preview, component workspace, and handoff reflect the same design decisions.","reason":"A change should carry through to the places a team actually uses, rather than depend on repeated manual updates.","tradeoff":"A designer needs to know which properties carry through and which need review before handoff."},{"title":"Let people inspect appearance and behaviour","choice":"The studio distinguishes controlled component specimens from interactive previews.","reason":"A design system needs comparable states and realistic behaviour. One view cannot do both equally well.","tradeoff":"Interactive examples add state and complexity; the underlying specimen must remain predictable for documentation and export."}],"outcome":"I shaped Arkitype around the relationship between foundations, component states, previews and handoff. The beta brings those surfaces into one workspace, with a documented library of 90 components. The design question I still want to test is whether a new user can follow a change through the whole system.","reflection":"I would ask a designer to change a foundation, find the affected component states and prepare a handoff. If they need a second explanation at every step, the workspace is still making them carry too much of the system in their head.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a designer to start from one brand colour, adjust a component, and produce a usable handoff. Record confusion, corrections, and output mismatches.","Proposed measure: time to the first coherent system and the number of inconsistencies discovered after export, with a defined task and baseline."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and lib/systemAreas.ts","detail":"Eight-area navigation, dependency graph, specimen/interactive distinction, beta scope and export pipeline."},{"label":"lib/componentLanes.ts and docs/GETTING_STARTED.md","detail":"Component registry and the documented first-use journey. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://arkitype.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/arkitype.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":3,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Change a token. [[See what it changes.]]","scene":"Imagine changing a brand colour and then checking every place it appears: components, documentation, previews, and the handoff. The change is small; keeping all its consequences aligned is the real work.","question":"What if the different outputs all came from the same decision?","insight":"A system needs [[relationships between decisions]], not just an inventory of components. Arkitype’s shared model connects foundations to the things a team can inspect and use.","pivot":"I organised the workspace around dependencies, letting designers revisit decisions while keeping their consequences visible.","takeaway":"System quality lives in what stays connected when something changes.","journey":[{"title":"Define","detail":"Set colour, type, space, shape, and motion."},{"title":"Bind","detail":"Connect component behaviour to system tokens."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"See the decisions in a real preview."},{"title":"Ship","detail":"Generate artifacts from the same model."}],"decisionLeads":["Let the work move in more than one direction.","Make one change travel through the system.","Show both the specimen and the behaviour."],"annotations":[{"title":"A working system","detail":"The archived capture introduces the actual product, rather than a speculative redesign."},{"title":"Follow a change","detail":"The palette and contrast inspector keep a foundation choice beside the information needed to evaluate it."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · shared model","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Designer shaping a shared system","context":"Revisiting foundations and component decisions during an evolving project.","need":"Understand how one decision affects the rest of the system.","friction":"Disconnected screens and handoff artifacts can drift.","implication":"Show relationships, states, and consequences together."}},"theme":{"layout":"system","mode":"light","paper":"#e8e8eb","ink":"#24242a","dim":"#5c5c68","line":"#bcbcc6","accent":"#4e3cad","wash":"#e1def2","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Space Grotesk","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A system in motion","process":"Relationships over inventory","decisions":"The workspace contract","outcome":"From decisions to handoff","reflection":"The first-use question"},"decisionHeading":"Keep design changes connected.","resultHeading":"One system, visible in several places."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/arkitype-workspace.jpg","alt":"Arkitype workspace showing foundation palettes, navigation and contrast inspector.","caption":"Foundations in context: the palette, navigation and contrast inspector share one workspace.","label":"Product interface","source":"Arkitype/docs/images/workspace.jpg","focus":[{"title":"Navigate the system","detail":"Foundations and components remain within reach."},{"title":"See the consequence","detail":"Editable palettes stay beside the result."},{"title":"Inspect the decision","detail":"Contrast information sits in the same workspace."}],"width":1280,"height":720,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":0,"y":60,"width":550,"height":360,"caption":"Foundations and components stay available in the workspace navigation.","alt":"Detail of the original Arkitype navigation beside the foundation palette."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"applecider","name":"AppleCider","headline":"Room to sketch before committing to a design."}},"applecider":{"slug":"applecider","name":"AppleCider","kind":"product","headline":"Room to sketch before committing to a design.","summary":"A sketch-like wireframing workspace that carries rough screens into clickable prototypes and editable handoff artifacts.","role":"Product idea, sketching workflow & interaction design","status":"Working product","context":"Design tools · Working product","facts":[{"value":"250","label":"documented library components"},{"value":"3","label":"sketch styles"},{"value":"Local","label":"browser-owned files"}],"problem":"Early design conversations can become arguments about polish before the underlying idea is settled. I framed AppleCider around an intentionally unfinished visual language: the speed and social permission of a sketch, with the editing capabilities of a digital workspace.","process":["I separated the experience into a welcoming introduction, a files workspace, and the editing canvas. This gives each moment a clear task: understand the tool, find the work, then explore an idea.","The design loop is sketch, revise, demonstrate, and hand off. Components, undo, snapping, links, and export matter because they let someone change their mind without starting again. Formal user validation remains a next step."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the unfinished quality deliberate","choice":"Wobbly strokes, a sketch-like type treatment, and three visual styles preserve a low-fidelity feel.","reason":"The interface should invite changes to the idea before encouraging arguments about pixel-level finish.","tradeoff":"A sketch aesthetic can also reduce legibility. Labels, focus states, and interactions still need the clarity of a serious tool."},{"title":"Keep the work portable","choice":"The files surface stores documents locally and supports a plain project-file export, alongside image and vector outputs.","reason":"A person should be able to keep and move their work without an account or a proprietary cloud workflow.","tradeoff":"Browser storage is device-specific and can be cleared. File ownership needs an understandable backup and export path."},{"title":"Carry structure into the next artifact","choice":"The handoff offers editable output and a written specification, alongside the clickable prototype.","reason":"The transition from a rough screen to a working conversation should preserve the decisions already made.","tradeoff":"Generated structure is a starting point. Placeholder copy and sketch styling should not be presented as finished product requirements."}],"outcome":"I used an intentionally rough visual language across the canvas, files workspace and prototype flow. The product includes 250 sketch components and editable handoff formats, so an early idea can move forward without pretending to be a finished interface.","reflection":"The roughness needs to make revision easier, not make the tool harder to use. I would test whether someone can change a screen, demonstrate its flow and hand it over without losing the decisions they already made.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: give participants an early product idea, ask them to revise it after feedback, and then explain the exported prototype to someone else.","Proposed measure: time to the first understandable screen, effort to revise its structure, and corrections needed after export."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and docs/architecture.md","detail":"Visual intent, component library, three surfaces, local file model and storage trade-off."},{"label":"src/io/export.ts and src/store/store.ts","detail":"Export and document-state behaviour supporting the documented workflow. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://applecider.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/applecider.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":6,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Room to sketch [[before committing to a design.]]","scene":"Picture an early design review drifting into a discussion about colour and spacing. The underlying idea is still changing, but the polished screen already feels too finished to challenge.","question":"Can a deliberately unfinished interface still have useful structure?","insight":"[[Low visual fidelity can preserve room to change]]. Clickable connections and portable outputs let a rough idea move into the next design conversation.","pivot":"I kept the sketch-like visual language and gave it the capabilities of a working design tool: move, revise, link, and hand off.","takeaway":"Commit to the structure only as fast as the idea earns it.","journey":[{"title":"Sketch","detail":"Get an idea onto a movable canvas."},{"title":"Revise","detail":"Change the structure while it still feels disposable."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Link the screens into a prototype."},{"title":"Carry forward","detail":"Export the structure for the next conversation."}],"decisionLeads":["Make unfinished feel intentional.","Give the person ownership of the work.","Carry the structure into the next artifact."],"annotations":[{"title":"The sketch is the point","detail":"The visual language makes the stage of the work clear."},{"title":"Structure underneath","detail":"Editable components and exports give the rough screen a useful next step."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · sketch and handoff workflow","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Designer discussing an early idea","context":"Exploring structure before investing in final visual polish.","need":"Make an idea concrete while keeping it easy to change.","friction":"A finished-looking mockup can make tentative decisions feel settled.","implication":"Keep the drawing rough and the interactions usable."}},"theme":{"layout":"sketchbook","mode":"light","paper":"#fffdf8","ink":"#2f2b25","dim":"#6c6256","line":"#d7cdbd","accent":"#a33a20","wash":"#f8e5d7","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"An idea still in pencil","process":"Keep it changeable","decisions":"Rough on purpose","outcome":"A sketch you can discuss","reflection":"What I would try with designers"},"decisionHeading":"Keep early ideas easy to revise.","resultHeading":"Rough enough to revise. Real enough to discuss."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/applecider.webp","alt":"AppleCider — archived product interface","caption":"The archived homepage demonstrates the intentionally rough visual language. These are the project’s own wireframe examples.","label":"Original wireframe examples","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"planit","name":"PlanIt","headline":"A trip plan that leaves room for people."}},"planit":{"slug":"planit","name":"PlanIt","kind":"product","headline":"A trip plan that leaves room for people.","summary":"A browser-based itinerary workspace for overlapping plans, different travellers, and different time zones.","role":"Product framing, group-planning journeys & interaction design","status":"Working product","context":"Group planning · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Shared","label":"plan across multiple views"},{"value":"Explicit","label":"board-to-time action"},{"value":"Local","label":"no account required"}],"problem":"A group trip is not a single ordered checklist. Different people arrive at different times, attend parallel activities, and move between places. I approached PlanIt as several useful views of one plan, with a board for thinking and a time model for checking whether that plan can work.","process":["I distinguished tentative ideas from scheduled commitments. The canvas supports exploration, while itinerary, timeline, map, and people views help the group understand the consequences of a plan.","The key interaction is the deliberate move from arrangement to schedule. Pins protect an existing commitment; loose ideas can stay undecided. The UX needs to explain what Resolve changes and make conflicts understandable."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give the board a clear but limited grammar","choice":"Day frames, ordering, and connectors convey meaning; loose cards can remain uncommitted ideas.","reason":"A planning surface needs room for uncertainty instead of assigning a date and time to everything immediately.","tradeoff":"Spatial meaning must be taught. A frame or connector that looks decorative can otherwise change a plan unexpectedly."},{"title":"Make scheduling an intentional action","choice":"Tidy turns the existing itinerary into a board; Resolve reads the board back into the schedule.","reason":"A person should know when arrangement becomes a commitment. Automatic rearrangement would undermine the value of a free board.","tradeoff":"Two explicit directions add a concept to learn, so the product needs visible feedback about what changed and why."},{"title":"Protect decisions the planner has already made","choice":"Pinned times win, untouched loose cards opt out, and resolution only moves times forward when constraints require it.","reason":"Automation should respect existing intent and explain an impossible plan rather than silently rewrite it.","tradeoff":"A conservative resolver may leave conflicts unresolved. The interface must help the person decide what to relax."}],"outcome":"I designed the board, itinerary, timeline, map and people views as different ways of working with the same trip. Scheduling is deliberate, pinned commitments stay protected, and conflicts remain visible. That behaviour is the part of PlanIt I would explain first to a new group.","reflection":"I would try the planner with a late arrival, two parallel activities and a booking that cannot move. The useful test is whether people can predict what Resolve will change and spot a conflict before it becomes a problem on the trip.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: plan a trip with parallel sessions, a pinned departure, and travellers in two zones; ask participants to predict Resolve before using it.","Proposed measure: unexpected schedule changes, correctly identified conflicts, and confidence explaining the final plan."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Planning views, board grammar, sharing model and scheduling constraints."},{"label":"src/core/resolve.ts and src/core/store.ts","detail":"Priority of pins, opt-in cards and forward-only resolution. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://planit.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/planit.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":9,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A trip plan [[that leaves room for people.]]","scene":"Imagine one person at a conference session, another arriving later, and a shared dinner across town. Put it all into one simple list and the useful differences disappear.","question":"When should a loose idea become a scheduled commitment?","insight":"A board needs room for uncertainty. [[Scheduling should be an explicit decision]], with existing commitments protected.","pivot":"I separated thinking on the board from committing to the schedule, with clear UX rules for what automation is allowed to change.","takeaway":"Useful automation makes its changes predictable.","journey":[{"title":"Arrange","detail":"Use frames and connectors to express intent."},{"title":"Protect","detail":"Keep pinned times as commitments."},{"title":"Resolve","detail":"Translate the arrangement into a schedule deliberately."},{"title":"Explain","detail":"Make remaining conflicts visible to the planner."}],"decisionLeads":["Let an idea stay an idea.","Make the moment of commitment visible.","Give automation a boundary it cannot cross."],"annotations":[{"title":"Several views, one plan","detail":"The working product carries the same trip across planning surfaces."},{"title":"An explicit contract","detail":"Pins, opt-in cards, and forward-only changes define the resolver."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · scheduling rules","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person coordinating a group trip","context":"Combining people with different arrivals, activities, and commitments.","need":"Turn possibilities into a plan while protecting confirmed times.","friction":"A single list hides overlaps and treats ideas as promises.","implication":"Separate tentative cards from explicit scheduling."}},"theme":{"layout":"planner","mode":"dark","paper":"#0d0f17","ink":"#edeff7","dim":"#a8b0c6","line":"#343e56","accent":"#ffb094","wash":"#35251f","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Instrument Sans","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"People with different plans","process":"Ideas are not commitments","decisions":"The scheduling contract","outcome":"The shared plan","reflection":"Where understanding can break"},"decisionHeading":"Separate ideas from confirmed plans.","resultHeading":"One plan, with room for different people."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/planit.webp","alt":"PlanIt — archived product interface","caption":"The archived PlanIt interface. Ideas, scheduled commitments and location are treated as different views of a shared trip.","label":"Planning workspace","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"earthlog","name":"Earthlog","headline":"What is happening around this place?"}},"earthlog":{"slug":"earthlog","name":"Earthlog","kind":"product","headline":"What is happening around this place?","summary":"Explore earthquakes, weather, and the night sky from one place on a globe.","role":"Experience framing, information hierarchy & map interactions","status":"Working product","context":"Data exploration · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"directions of exploration"},{"value":"Place-led","label":"event context"},{"value":"Labelled","label":"models and estimates"}],"problem":"A place can have recent earthquakes, old incident records, current weather, and predicted sky events. Bringing them onto one map makes exploration easier, but can also make very different information look equally current and reliable.","process":["I framed two ways into exploration: start from a place, or follow an interesting event to its location. Both routes should arrive at a readable local picture, rather than another layer to decode.","Freshness and uncertainty are part of the information hierarchy. An observation, an older open record, and a forecast need different labels so the map does not make them look equally current or certain."],"decisions":[{"title":"Start with a place or a question","choice":"People can search for a place, select the globe, or follow an event shortcut.","reason":"Someone may be curious about the latest earthquake without knowing where it happened.","tradeoff":"Every route needs to lead back to the same place context."},{"title":"Show when the information was observed","choice":"The documented event model uses observation recency instead of treating every open incident as current.","reason":"A source’s administrative status can outlive the actual event. The label should communicate what is known.","tradeoff":"Freshness rules remain approximations; source links and timestamps must stay visible."},{"title":"Explain the limits beside the result","choice":"Eclipse paths, station passes, and aurora layers carry model limitations in the documented product.","reason":"A compelling map should not appear more precise than the calculation behind it.","tradeoff":"Caveats can overwhelm the visual story, so they belong beside the relevant result rather than only in documentation."}],"outcome":"I brought place search and event discovery together on the globe. A visitor can start with a location or an event, then inspect dates, sources and the relevant model limits. I kept that context close to the result rather than making the globe’s appearance carry the explanation.","reflection":"I would ask someone to explain one result in their own words: what happened, when it was observed and where the information came from. Finding an event is only part of the task.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: ask visitors to distinguish a recent observation from a modelled forecast and locate the source behind each result.","Proposed measure: correct interpretation of freshness and uncertainty, alongside success finding a relevant local event."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What it answers and Honesty notes","detail":"Bidirectional exploration, source freshness and model limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://earthlog.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/earthlog.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":12,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"What is happening [[around this place?]]","scene":"Imagine looking at a place on a globe and wanting more than a pin: the latest quake, tonight’s sky, or the weather around it. Those answers arrive from different sources with different levels of certainty.","question":"What is happening here—and how current is the information?","insight":"An incident can remain open after the last observation. [[A status label is not enough]] to explain whether something is happening now.","pivot":"I kept dates, sources, and model limits alongside the result, so visitors can judge the information while exploring.","takeaway":"Make the answer easy to find and easy to question.","journey":[{"title":"Choose","detail":"Start with a place or an interesting event."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Read the local context across sources."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"See when the information was observed and how it was derived."}],"decisionLeads":["Offer a question as well as a place.","Make freshness more than a status label.","Keep uncertainty beside the answer."],"annotations":[{"title":"A place-led dossier","detail":"The capture shows how the globe connects to local information."},{"title":"An explanation with boundaries","detail":"Model limits remain part of the result."}],"evidenceTag":"Based on the documented freshness rules and source labels.","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"People exploring a place or following an event","context":"Following an event or asking what is happening near a location.","need":"Find local context and understand how current it is.","friction":"Different observations and forecasts can appear equally certain on a map.","implication":"Keep timestamps, sources, and uncertainty beside the result."}},"theme":{"layout":"atlas","mode":"dark","paper":"#06080b","ink":"#ece9e3","dim":"#b6bac1","line":"#454b55","accent":"#f2a93b","wash":"#33271a","bodyFont":"Public Sans","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Start with a place","process":"Keep the date beside the answer","decisions":"Reading an uncertain world","outcome":"The local picture","reflection":"What the map implies"},"decisionHeading":"Three choices behind the exploration","resultHeading":"One place to explore several kinds of information"},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/earthlog.webp","alt":"Earthlog — archived product interface","caption":"The actual exploration surface: globe and event entry points on the left; place, weather, sky and history in the inspector.","label":"Globe and place inspector","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","focus":[{"title":"Enter through a place","detail":"Search and globe selection start from a location."},{"title":"Enter through an event","detail":"Event shortcuts reverse the route into the same place context."},{"title":"Read in context","detail":"The inspector separates place, weather, sky and history."}],"width":1280,"height":720,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":14,"y":488,"width":500,"height":192,"caption":"Event shortcuts let a visitor start with a question before choosing a place.","alt":"Original Earthlog event shortcuts for earthquakes, eclipses, the space station and other events."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"hexchess","name":"HexChess","headline":"Learning chess on a different board."}},"hexchess":{"slug":"hexchess","name":"HexChess","kind":"product","headline":"Learning chess on a different board.","summary":"A 91-cell chess variant designed around guided learning, readable interaction, and games that survive interruptions.","role":"Learning journey, onboarding & game interactions","status":"Working product","context":"Learning & play · Working product","facts":[{"value":"91","label":"board cells"},{"value":"6","label":"guided piece lessons"},{"value":"Offline","label":"after first successful load"}],"problem":"A hexagonal board changes movement patterns enough to make familiar chess knowledge unreliable. I designed HexChess around learning by trying: clear targets, feedback on moves, and a route into untimed practice instead of expecting a beginner to absorb a rules document first.","process":["I connected a six-part introduction to the first practice game. A learner should encounter a rule on the board, try a move, and carry that understanding into play.","I treated interruption and sharing as parts of the experience. A saved game needs a clear return path, and a correspondence link needs to explain that the other person replies with an updated game rather than joins a live room."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make movement teachable on the board","choice":"Lessons give a specific target, neutral markers, retries, and progress based on actual practice.","reason":"A learner can connect a rule to a move instead of memorising abstract directions.","tradeoff":"Move previews and legal moves serve different purposes; the interface must explain when inspection ignores king safety."},{"title":"Keep assistance appropriate to the context","choice":"Hints and takebacks belong to computer practice; human games remove assistance and pause controls.","reason":"Learning support should not silently change the rules of a friendly competitive game.","tradeoff":"Assistance rules need to be clear to both players; the interface is not evidence of tournament-grade enforcement."},{"title":"Describe sharing accurately","choice":"Correspondence links carry move history and require an updated reply after a turn.","reason":"People should understand whether they are entering a synchronized room or receiving a game snapshot.","tradeoff":"Long histories create long URLs, and a shared snapshot is readable by anyone given the link."}],"outcome":"I connected the 91-cell board to six guided lessons, practice, saved sessions and correspondence sharing. Legal-move feedback helps explain the unfamiliar geometry where the move happens. I have not measured whether the lessons improve learning.","reflection":"I would follow a new player from a lesson into their first game. A completed tutorial is less useful evidence than seeing them recognise a move, recover from a mistake and continue without an explanation.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: observe a new player complete a piece lesson and use the same movement unaided in practice.","Proposed checks: reload during a game, resume offline, and exchange a correspondence reply while confirming both players understand the snapshot model."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Learning, board interactions, saves, offline support, sharing boundaries and existing test scope. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hexchess.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/hexchess-live.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":15,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Learning chess [[on a different board.]]","scene":"Imagine recognising the pieces but no longer trusting your instinct for where they can move. A rules page can explain the geometry; a first move makes it tangible.","question":"How can the board become part of the teaching?","insight":"[[Practice makes a rule concrete]]. Six guided piece lessons lead into an untimed first game with move highlights.","pivot":"I brought explanation into the interaction: a target, an attempt, feedback, and a recoverable retry.","takeaway":"Teach the next useful action, then give someone room to try it.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Give the lesson one clear target."},{"title":"Try","detail":"Let the learner select and move."},{"title":"Recover","detail":"Explain a wrong attempt without ending the lesson."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Transfer the movement into beginner practice."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the rule visible where it is used.","Keep practice help appropriate to the game.","Describe what a shared game really is."],"annotations":[{"title":"The board stays primary","detail":"The playing room keeps the central interaction prominent."},{"title":"Progress survives interruption","detail":"Local saves and offline support belong to the game experience."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · six guided lessons","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Chess player learning a different board","context":"Recognising familiar pieces in an unfamiliar hexagonal geometry.","need":"Understand a movement rule and try it safely.","friction":"Abstract directions do not automatically translate into a playable move.","implication":"Teach on the board, then open guided practice."}},"theme":{"layout":"lesson","mode":"light","paper":"#f6f6f3","ink":"#32363b","dim":"#62696e","line":"#d2d5d6","accent":"#46584c","wash":"#e1e7db","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Libre Caslon Display","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A familiar piece, a new board","process":"Learning through a move","decisions":"From hint to understanding","outcome":"The first game","reflection":"What practice would reveal"},"decisionHeading":"Teach the board through the next move.","resultHeading":"From the first lesson into play."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/hexchess-live.webp","alt":"HexChess — archived product interface","caption":"The original playing room: a 91-cell board, legal-move guidance and game controls.","label":"Playing interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","focus":[{"title":"Understand the board","detail":"The playing surface carries the movement explanation."},{"title":"Try a move","detail":"Guidance stays in the context of play."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Practice and a saved game support a return to the board."}],"width":1265,"height":949},"nextStory":{"slug":"dashf1","name":"DashF1","headline":"Follow the driver. Keep the race in view."}},"dashf1":{"slug":"dashf1","name":"DashF1","kind":"product","headline":"Follow the driver. Keep the race in view.","summary":"A race dashboard connecting timing, track context, and replay while distinguishing measured data from estimated positions.","role":"Dashboard hierarchy, driver focus & information design","status":"Working product","context":"Live data · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"driver focus across panels"},{"value":"Live / replay","label":"distinct data modes"},{"value":"Estimated","label":"free-feed track positions"}],"problem":"A race weekend produces more signals than a person can follow at once, and the available feed changes between live and replay sessions. DashF1 uses one shared driver focus across the timing tower, map, and detail views so the dashboard supports a question rather than a wall of independent charts.","process":["I organised the experience around one driver focus. Timing, track context, and detail panels should answer related questions without requiring the same selection repeatedly.","I separated live and replay expectations. When a position is estimated, the label belongs near the moving marker; missing coverage should be understandable at the moment someone tries to use it."],"decisions":[{"title":"Carry one selection through the dashboard","choice":"Choosing a driver updates the relevant map and telemetry context.","reason":"The person should not have to repeat the same selection in several panels.","tradeoff":"The relationship must remain clear when the selected driver retires or a particular data channel is unavailable."},{"title":"Let the mode determine the promise","choice":"Live mode shows supported channels; fuller replay data becomes available through a separate path.","reason":"Missing data should change the interface’s promise instead of being replaced with plausible-looking numbers.","tradeoff":"A mode change can alter familiar panels, so the dashboard needs an understandable explanation."},{"title":"Label an estimate at the point of use","choice":"The documented free-feed map derives position from mini-sector timing and gaps, and calls it estimated.","reason":"Visual continuity is useful, but it should not be mistaken for location precision the feed does not provide.","tradeoff":"Race interruptions and stale timing can challenge the estimate; smooth motion must not conceal those limits."}],"outcome":"I organised timing, track position, telemetry and replay around one selected driver. The selection carries across panels, while session mode and data coverage explain what each view can show. Estimated positions need to stay visibly different from recorded data.","reflection":"I would check what someone believes when a feed is incomplete. An empty or estimated view should be understandable without making them leave the race to read a technical explanation.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a viewer to follow a battle and explain which values are observed, historical, or estimated.","Proposed checks: missing telemetry, stale updates, interrupted streams, safety-car periods, and the transition back to replay."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What’s on screen and Race mode","detail":"Layout, shared focus, data-source boundaries and position-estimation limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"Local built F1DASH, captured 31 October 2026; historical replay, not a live session."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/dashf1-replay.webp","alt":"DashF1 actual Monaco 2025 historical replay with timing, track, telemetry and playback controls","caption":"Actual local dashboard replaying the 2025 Monaco race from OpenF1 data. The prediction panel labels its heuristic estimates.","width":1280,"height":720}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://f1dash.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/dashf1-replay.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":18,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Follow the driver. [[Keep the race in view.]]","scene":"Imagine following two drivers through a race weekend. Timing, position, tyres, and telemetry compete for attention—and not every channel is available in every session.","question":"What should the dashboard do when its data coverage changes?","insight":"A smooth marker can still be an estimate. [[The mode should determine the promise]] the interface makes.","pivot":"I used one driver focus across the dashboard and separate live/replay modes that respect the available information.","takeaway":"A useful dashboard can say less when it knows less.","journey":[{"title":"Focus","detail":"Choose the driver once."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Carry that focus across panels."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"Distinguish measured channels from estimated positions."}],"decisionLeads":["Carry one question across the dashboard.","Let the available data set the scope.","Make a moving estimate visibly an estimate."],"annotations":[{"title":"Shared focus","detail":"A selected driver carries across timing, track position and available detail."},{"title":"Coverage has boundaries","detail":"The product distinguishes live estimates from richer replay data."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · live and replay modes","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Race follower tracking a driver","context":"Moving between timing, track position, and available detail.","need":"Keep one driver in context while following the race.","friction":"Repeated selections and uneven data coverage disrupt the story.","implication":"Synchronise focus and explain each mode’s coverage."}},"theme":{"layout":"race","mode":"dark","paper":"#0b0e14","ink":"#eef1f6","dim":"#b3bccb","line":"#394454","accent":"#ff6a61","wash":"#3a1e24","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Space Grotesk","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A driver to follow","process":"One focus across panels","decisions":"The dashboard contract","outcome":"The race in context","reflection":"Knowing what is estimated"},"decisionHeading":"Keep one driver in focus across the dashboard.","resultHeading":"Follow the driver through the available data."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/dashf1-replay.webp","presentation":"spread","alt":"DashF1 actual Monaco 2025 historical replay with timing, track, telemetry and playback controls","caption":"Actual local dashboard replaying the 2025 Monaco race from OpenF1 data. The prediction panel labels its heuristic estimates.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"Local built F1DASH, captured 31 October 2026; historical replay, not a live session.","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"hued","name":"Hued","headline":"One colour. A reason to look closer."}},"hued":{"slug":"hued","name":"Hued","kind":"product","headline":"One colour. A reason to look closer.","summary":"A daily photography prompt with a shared constraint, on-device scoring, and a warm visual language.","role":"Product concept, creative loop & visual direction","status":"Mobile beta","context":"Creative habits · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"shared daily colour"},{"value":"48","label":"colours in the documented palette"},{"value":"On-device","label":"colour scoring"}],"problem":"A broad prompt to be creative can be harder to act on than a small constraint. Hued gives everyone the same daily colour and asks them to find and photograph it in the real world. The product connects a simple creative action to a repeatable daily rhythm.","process":["I shaped the concept around one shared colour and a small creative invitation: notice it in the world, photograph it, and see other interpretations. The daily boundary needs a clear local explanation.","A quiet, warm interface keeps the photographs in front. Scores and streaks are supporting feedback; whether they encourage curiosity or pressure is a question for evaluation, not an established habit-formation result."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the prompt shared and specific","choice":"One date-derived colour creates a common constraint for the day.","reason":"A small, concrete invitation can make starting easier and make different responses interesting to compare.","tradeoff":"A UTC boundary may feel surprising across time zones; the deadline needs to be understandable locally."},{"title":"Keep the creative result in the foreground","choice":"The feed emphasises photographs within a quiet, warm visual system.","reason":"The person’s interpretation of the colour should carry more attention than surrounding interface decoration.","tradeoff":"Ranking and streaks can compete with exploration; their value needs to be judged from behaviour and feedback."},{"title":"Treat scoring as guidance with limits","choice":"Colour matching runs locally; suspicious-image heuristics are documented as tentative rather than proof.","reason":"A score can provide feedback without pretending to reliably determine how an image was made.","tradeoff":"Lighting, camera processing, and scene composition affect colour. An unexplained penalty can feel arbitrary."}],"outcome":"I centred the app on a shared daily colour, a photograph and feedback on the attempt. The warm interface gives the photographs most of the attention; scores, the feed and streaks support the daily activity. Participation and the scoring experience still need user evaluation.","reflection":"I want the prompt to encourage noticing things, not just protecting a streak. I would ask people what they chose to photograph and how they interpreted the score before deciding whether to make the competitive features more prominent.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: compare how people interpret the prompt and score under different lighting conditions, including whether they understand a low score.","Proposed measure: repeat participation with self-reported creative value; evaluate deadline understanding across time zones."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/dailyColor.ts","detail":"Daily prompt, palette and beta scope."},{"label":"src/lib/scoring.ts and README limitations","detail":"On-device colour evaluation and best-effort heuristics. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"Hued release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hued-welcome.webp","alt":"Hued native Android sign-in screen with email, password and sign-up controls","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. The daily photography flow is described from the project documentation; no account or photographs were used for this capture.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hued.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/hued-welcome.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":21,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"One colour. [[A reason to look closer.]]","scene":"Imagine being asked to make something creative today. A blank prompt is wide open; one findable colour gives your attention somewhere specific to go.","question":"How much structure helps creativity begin?","insight":"[[A small shared constraint]] gives the day a common starting point without prescribing the photograph.","pivot":"I connected the daily colour to finding, photographing, and receiving feedback, while keeping the photographs central.","takeaway":"A good prompt opens possibilities by making the first step small.","journey":[{"title":"Notice","detail":"Find the shared colour in the world."},{"title":"Capture","detail":"Make your own interpretation."},{"title":"Reflect","detail":"Use the feedback as guidance, with its limits visible."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the invitation specific.","Keep the creative result in the foreground.","Let a score explain rather than overrule."],"annotations":[{"title":"One daily invitation","detail":"The product identity anchors a shared colour challenge."},{"title":"Interpretation stays personal","detail":"Local scoring is feedback, not a verdict on creativity."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · daily prompt and scoring","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person looking for a small creative prompt","context":"Noticing everyday surroundings through one daily colour.","need":"Find a subject and share a personal interpretation.","friction":"Scores and streaks can turn exploration into pressure.","implication":"Let the photograph lead and keep feedback supportive."}},"theme":{"layout":"photojournal","mode":"light","paper":"#fffaf6","ink":"#2f2927","dim":"#75615b","line":"#e8d5ca","accent":"#ad4b35","wash":"#f8e2d7","bodyFont":"Montserrat","displayFont":"Montserrat","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A colour in the everyday","process":"A constraint with room for you","decisions":"Designing the daily invitation","outcome":"Notice, photograph, reflect","reflection":"Creativity before streaks"},"decisionHeading":"Make the daily prompt easy to act on.","resultHeading":"A little colour, interpreted differently."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hued-welcome.webp","presentation":"portrait","alt":"Hued native Android sign-in screen with email, password and sign-up controls","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. The daily photography flow is described from the project documentation; no account or photographs were used for this capture.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"Hued release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026.","width":1080,"height":1920},"nextStory":{"slug":"yuva-panaji","name":"Yuva Panaji","headline":"An event page is only the beginning."}},"yuva-panaji":{"slug":"yuva-panaji","name":"Yuva Panaji","kind":"product","headline":"An event page is only the beginning.","summary":"A community website and operations portal linking event discovery with registration and committee workflows.","role":"Information architecture, registration & volunteer workflows","status":"Working system","context":"Community operations · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"connected surfaces"},{"value":"Public","label":"events and registration"},{"value":"Private","label":"committee operations"}],"problem":"A community organisation needs a public account of what it does and a practical way to run the next event. The reviewed Yuva source brings those responsibilities together: public discovery and registration on one side, event administration and committee workflows on the other.","process":["I mapped the service from public event discovery and registration through committee review and the attendance desk. Participants and volunteers need different views of the same event information.","The design priority is continuity: information entered on a registration form should remain useful when a volunteer checks someone in, corrects a record, or prepares a printed list."],"decisions":[{"title":"Connect discovery to the event’s operational record","choice":"The public registration journey and admin roster belong to the same event model.","reason":"A committee should not have to reinterpret a public sign-up into a separate attendance workflow.","tradeoff":"Custom events need flexible fields without making every registration form equally complex."},{"title":"Give public and committee users different surfaces","choice":"Visitors see event and organisation information, while management workflows sit behind admin access.","reason":"The interface should reflect different responsibilities and information needs.","tradeoff":"A clean separation in navigation is only part of the requirement; permissions and data handling must be verified independently."},{"title":"Include the work that happens away from the screen","choice":"The documented system supports desk attendance sheets and spreadsheet exports.","reason":"Community operations often happen at an event desk, where a printable list can be more useful than another dashboard.","tradeoff":"Exported files can become stale. The workflow should explain when a printout was produced and how corrections return to the system."}],"outcome":"I connected public event information and registration with the committee’s follow-up work: rosters, corrections, exports and attendance. The public website and operations portal have different jobs, but the information needs to carry from one to the other.","reflection":"I would review the handoff at an actual event: who checks a registration, who corrects it and what the attendance desk receives. That is where duplicate entry and outdated lists become visible.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: follow one event from discovery through registration, roster correction, and check-in with both participant and committee perspectives.","Proposed measure: duplicate corrections, incomplete registrations, and handoff effort at the event desk, with a documented baseline."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/db.js","detail":"Current public/admin architecture, event operations and Supabase-backed data layer. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://yuva-website.netlify.app/","cover":"assets/products/yuva.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":24,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"An event page [[is only the beginning.]]","scene":"Imagine someone finding an event and registering. Their next step is attendance. For the committee, that same action starts a chain of rosters, corrections, desk sheets, and follow-up.","question":"Can one action stay useful all the way to the event desk?","insight":"[[The public journey and the operational record]] are parts of the same service. The handoff matters as much as the page.","pivot":"I mapped how public discovery and registration continue into committee workflows, including the work that happens away from a screen.","takeaway":"Design the next person’s task into the current person’s action.","journey":[{"title":"Discover","detail":"Find the event and its details."},{"title":"Register","detail":"Submit the information the event actually needs."},{"title":"Prepare","detail":"Carry it into a usable committee roster."},{"title":"Check in","detail":"Support attendance at the event desk."}],"decisionLeads":["Connect the action to the record behind it.","Give each responsibility its own surface.","Design for the handoff beyond the screen."],"annotations":[{"title":"A public front door","detail":"The community website introduces the organisation and its work."},{"title":"An operational continuation","detail":"The case follows the event into registration and committee tasks."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · public and admin workflows","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Event participant & committee volunteer","context":"One person registers; another prepares the roster and attendance desk.","need":"Carry the same event information from discovery into check-in.","friction":"Public sign-ups and operational lists can become disconnected.","implication":"Design the participant and volunteer journeys together."}},"theme":{"layout":"service","mode":"light","paper":"#fbfaf5","ink":"#22301f","dim":"#5c6855","line":"#d8ddce","accent":"#157a45","wash":"#e3eedc","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Two sides of one event","process":"Registration continues at the desk","decisions":"Designing the whole service","outcome":"From public page to roster","reflection":"Following real event work"},"decisionHeading":"Connect public events to the work behind them.","resultHeading":"The public event and the work behind it."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/yuva.webp","alt":"Yuva Panaji — archived product interface","caption":"Original public homepage for Yuva Panaji. Community activity introduces the organisation before visitors enter its event and registration routes.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"deckforge","name":"DeckForge","headline":"A PDF deck you can work with again."}},"deckforge":{"slug":"deckforge","name":"DeckForge","kind":"product","headline":"A PDF deck you can work with again.","summary":"A browser presentation tool that turns PDF drawing instructions into editable objects, animation, and presenting workflows.","role":"Product idea, editing experience & presentation flows","status":"Working product","context":"Presentation tools · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Editable","label":"reconstructed page objects"},{"value":"40","label":"documented animation presets"},{"value":"Browser","label":"core document processing"}],"problem":"A PDF preserves how a slide looks but usually loses the convenient objects a presenter wants to edit. DeckForge approaches that gap as a reconstruction problem: recover meaningful text, images, and vector objects, then let a person work with them rather than repainting a screenshot.","process":["I framed the editor around objects a presenter can recognise: a text block, a logo, a shape, or a chart. A technically selectable fragment is not useful if its meaning is unclear.","The flow needs to explain what can be edited, preserve a usable slide when recovery is limited, and distinguish live presentation pacing from an exported recording."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make layers correspond to recognisable objects","choice":"Paths are grouped into likely logos, dividers, shapes, and chart series, while text is reconstructed into blocks.","reason":"An editable layer is useful only if the person recognises what it represents.","tradeoff":"Object recovery is heuristic. Preserving appearance can be more valuable than forcing misleading editability."},{"title":"Let a presenter control the pace","choice":"Animations can wait for an advance action, while timed outputs play sequentially.","reason":"A live talk and an exported recording need different behaviour from the same slide.","tradeoff":"That difference must be visible before export so the presenter knows what an audience will receive."},{"title":"Keep the core document workflow local","choice":"PDF reconstruction and editing run in the browser; phone pairing has a separate connection mechanism.","reason":"Local processing supports a lightweight workflow and avoids requiring a document-upload service.","tradeoff":"Phone pairing still uses signalling, and some remote features exchange preview frames. The privacy explanation should distinguish those paths."}],"outcome":"I organised recovered slide content into recognisable objects that can be edited, sequenced, presented and exported. Some content remains an image, so the interface needs to explain what was recovered and what can actually be changed.","reflection":"I would use a difficult deck to test the explanation, not just a clean example. Someone should be able to tell why one element is editable and another is not, then choose a workable next step.","nextSteps":["Proposed benchmark: import a defined set of native-text, scanned, multi-column, and dense-vector PDFs; record editability and visible fidelity separately.","Proposed study: revise a real slide and present it, measuring manual corrections and surprises between editor, audience view, and exported output."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, How extraction works and Known limitations","detail":"Reconstruction pipeline, presentation model, export boundaries and remote-pairing caveat."},{"label":"src/engine and src/store","detail":"Shared extraction, animation, export and document-state behaviour. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://deckforge.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/deckforge.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":27,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A PDF deck [[you can work with again.]]","scene":"Imagine needing to revise one text block or animate one diagram, but having only the exported PDF. The visual is preserved; the convenient objects are gone.","question":"What does it take to recover something a presenter can actually work with?","insight":"[[Recognisable objects make editability useful]]. Text, paths, and images need to become meaningful layers rather than a pile of drawing instructions.","pivot":"I framed the journey around recognising an object, revising it, and presenting it, with an explanation when recovered content cannot be edited.","takeaway":"Recover enough structure to make the next action possible.","journey":[{"title":"Recover","detail":"Read text, paths, and images from the PDF."},{"title":"Group","detail":"Reassemble objects the person recognises."},{"title":"Revise","detail":"Edit and sequence meaningful layers."},{"title":"Present","detail":"Carry the same slide model into the talk."}],"decisionLeads":["Recover the object a person recognises.","Let the presenter control the pace.","Explain what stays local and what connects."],"annotations":[{"title":"A working reconstruction tool","detail":"The capture introduces the actual editor workflow."},{"title":"An honest fallback","detail":"Scanned or dense pages can remain usable raster layers."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · slide editing journey","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Presenter revising an existing PDF","context":"Making changes shortly before sharing or presenting a deck.","need":"Recognise editable content and control presentation pacing.","friction":"A recovered fragment may be selectable without being meaningful.","implication":"Expose recognisable objects and explain editing limits."}},"theme":{"layout":"slides","mode":"dark","paper":"#17191c","ink":"#f7f8fa","dim":"#b2bac6","line":"#545c67","accent":"#b9aeff","wash":"#302b46","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A deck that needs changing","process":"Objects should be recognisable","decisions":"Designing edit and present","outcome":"The recovered slide","reflection":"When a page stays an image"},"decisionHeading":"Make the PDF’s editable parts understandable.","resultHeading":"A slide that can keep moving."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/deckforge.webp","alt":"DeckForge — archived product interface","caption":"Original DeckForge starting screen for opening a PDF presentation. Editing and animation begin with an existing deck.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1269,"height":714},"nextStory":{"slug":"vansh-vriksha","name":"Vansh Vriksha","headline":"Family connections with a source to follow."}},"vansh-vriksha":{"slug":"vansh-vriksha","name":"Vansh Vriksha","kind":"product","headline":"Family connections with a source to follow.","summary":"A community genealogy archive that treats relationships, provenance, and contribution review as parts of the same experience.","role":"Archive concept, relationship exploration & contribution UX","status":"Working archive","context":"Community knowledge · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Graph","label":"relationship model"},{"value":"Provenance","label":"per-record intent"},{"value":"Reviewed","label":"contribution workflow"}],"problem":"A family archive is not simply a diagram of names. Relationships can be disputed, spellings vary, and historical records represent some people much better than others. Vansh Vriksha is structured around exploring those connections while preserving the basis for a record.","process":["I treated the archive as a set of connected records with several entry points: a person, a family, a village, or a relationship. The UX should help someone follow a connection without losing its source.","The demonstration content lets the experience be explored; it is not verified family evidence. Missing records, uncertain relationships, and contribution review need visible explanations."],"decisions":[{"title":"Model relationships beyond a simple tree","choice":"The graph includes family unions and parent links rather than forcing every connection into one hierarchy.","reason":"A plain tree can misrepresent remarriage and half-sibling relationships. The data model needs to preserve the actual relationship.","tradeoff":"A more faithful model is harder to lay out and explain, especially on small screens."},{"title":"Keep a record’s basis alongside the record","choice":"The documented model includes provenance and versioned family files.","reason":"Someone reading a family claim should have a way to understand where it came from and how it changed.","tradeoff":"A source field does not guarantee accuracy. Disagreement and corrections still need a human review process."},{"title":"Separate contribution from publication","choice":"Visitors can submit without an account, while a maintainer reviews additions.","reason":"A low-friction contribution path can coexist with an accountable published archive.","tradeoff":"Moderation becomes an operational commitment; the interface should communicate what happens while a submission waits."}],"outcome":"I designed relationship browsing, record context and contribution review as connected parts of the archive. A person can explore a connection and inspect the basis for it. The demonstration records show the interaction; they do not establish verified family histories.","reflection":"I would make gaps in the archive easier to recognise. A missing branch can mean incomplete records, so it should not quietly look like a statement about which families or people matter.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: ask a family contributor to submit a relationship, inspect its provenance, and correct a disputed connection.","Proposed review: distinguish demonstration records from sourced records in every public count and assess living-person privacy across search and sharing."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/archive.ts","detail":"Versioned record storage, graph exploration, moderation, demonstration data and known biases. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://vanshvriksha.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/vansh-live.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":30,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Family connections [[with a source to follow.]]","scene":"Imagine recognising a relative in an archive and wanting to know how the relationship was recorded. The diagram can show a connection, but it also needs a basis you can inspect.","question":"How can an archive invite contribution without pretending every claim is settled?","insight":"[[Provenance belongs with the relationship]]. A family graph, versioned records, and review make different parts of that promise concrete.","pivot":"I connected exploration with source-aware records and a contribution path that separates submitting from publishing.","takeaway":"An archive should show what it knows and how it came to know it.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Follow the connections through the archive."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Read the basis for a record."},{"title":"Contribute","detail":"Submit a correction or a family link."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Publish after a maintainer’s review."}],"decisionLeads":["Model the relationship faithfully.","Keep the basis of a claim close.","Let a contribution wait for review."],"annotations":[{"title":"Multiple ways into a family history","detail":"The public experience offers search and relationship exploration."},{"title":"Coverage stays conditional","detail":"Demonstration records and historical coverage limits need context."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · graph and review model","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Family member exploring a connection","context":"Following names and relationships with incomplete historical information.","need":"Understand a relationship and the evidence behind it.","friction":"A convincing diagram can imply certainty or completeness.","implication":"Keep sources and contribution review near the record."}},"theme":{"layout":"archive","mode":"light","paper":"#fbf6ec","ink":"#37271f","dim":"#78664e","line":"#ddcfb6","accent":"#a8322a","wash":"#f6e2dc","bodyFont":"Archivo","displayFont":"Newsreader","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Following a family connection","process":"A connection needs a source","decisions":"Designing a careful archive","outcome":"The record and its relationships","reflection":"What the archive leaves out"},"decisionHeading":"Keep the evidence behind a relationship visible.","resultHeading":"A family connection with its context intact."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/vansh-live.webp","alt":"Vansh Vriksha — archived product interface","caption":"The original archive interface introduces people, families and the connections between records.","label":"Archive interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"premoney","name":"PreMoney","headline":"A bank message is not always a clean transaction."}},"premoney":{"slug":"premoney","name":"PreMoney","kind":"product","headline":"A bank message is not always a clean transaction.","summary":"A personal-finance app that converts bank and UPI messages into reviewable transactions, categories, and summaries.","role":"Product concept, transaction review & correction flows","status":"Interactive app","context":"Personal finance · Working product","facts":[{"value":"SMS","label":"transaction input"},{"value":"Confidence","label":"visible in parser model"},{"value":"Review","label":"unresolved merchants"}],"problem":"Manual expense entry asks people to repeat information already present in transaction alerts. PreMoney explores a more useful starting point: extract likely transactions from messages, then make uncertainty and correction part of the workflow.","process":["I framed the core journey as evidence, suggested transaction, review, correction, and summary. A person should be able to understand why an alert became a record and recover when the interpretation is wrong.","Privacy explanations belong alongside optional categorisation and synchronisation. The experience needs to make those choices understandable without relying on a blanket claim that all processing stays on the device."],"decisions":[{"title":"Prefer an uncertain result to a fabricated detail","choice":"The parser lowers confidence when a field is uncertain and records when a merchant is unresolved.","reason":"A plausible but incorrect financial record can be harder to notice than a clear request for review.","tradeoff":"Conservative parsing can miss legitimate alerts. The product needs a recoverable route for false rejections."},{"title":"Make correction useful beyond a single transaction","choice":"Transaction editing and review make suggested categories and corrections inspectable.","reason":"Repeated mistakes should become easier to diagnose than manually editing the same pattern forever.","tradeoff":"A learned rule can also propagate a wrong assumption, so its effect must remain inspectable."},{"title":"Treat enrichment as a separate data decision","choice":"Optional categorisation and synchronisation need a separate explanation from reading a transaction alert.","reason":"People need an understandable account of which data is used by which feature.","tradeoff":"The product documentation describes external processing paths; disclosures and controls need review before making privacy promises."}],"outcome":"I designed a route from the original message to a suggested transaction, correction and summary. The important part is being able to inspect an entry’s origin and fix it when the interpretation is wrong. Accuracy needs to be evaluated against a defined set of messages.","reflection":"I would start with a wrong amount, a duplicate alert and a message that is not a transaction. If correcting those cases is difficult, adding more charts will not make the financial record more dependable.","nextSteps":["Proposed benchmark: use a consented, redacted alert corpus to measure false accepts, false rejects, duplicate errors, and corrections by bank format.","Proposed release review: document the external-processing and sync paths, then test whether a person understands and controls them."],"sources":[{"label":"lib/domain/sms/sms_parser.dart and lib/domain/categorize","detail":"Confidence model, rejection reasons and merchant categorisation."},{"label":"lib/domain/ai/groq_service.dart and lib/ui/settings/sms_sandbox_sheet.dart","detail":"External enrichment payload and diagnostic review surface. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"PreMoney 1.13.0 release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026. No bank messages or personal transactions."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/premoney-dashboard.webp","alt":"PreMoney actual Android dashboard showing its empty state and insufficient-data scores","caption":"Actual Android dashboard with no transactions. Scores stay unavailable until there is enough information; SMS access was skipped for this capture.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/PreMoney-releases","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/premoney-dashboard.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":33,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A bank message [[is not always a clean transaction.]]","scene":"Imagine an expense app reading a bank alert. Automation has saved an entry—but if it guessed the amount or merchant, the summary now looks more certain than its input.","question":"How should automatic tracking admit that it is unsure?","insight":"[[A reviewable uncertainty is useful information]]. Confidence, rejection reasons, and unresolved merchants give correction a place in the workflow.","pivot":"I framed the journey around inspecting and correcting a suggested transaction, with separate explanations for optional categorisation and sync.","takeaway":"Automation earns trust by making its uncertainty correctable.","journey":[{"title":"Read","detail":"Distinguish an account movement from other alerts."},{"title":"Flag","detail":"Keep uncertainty in the transaction model."},{"title":"Correct","detail":"Inspect the origin and improve the record."}],"decisionLeads":["Leave uncertainty visible.","Make a correction useful beyond one row.","Explain enrichment as its own data decision."],"annotations":[{"title":"Confidence is a design input","detail":"Ambiguous transaction alerts need an explicit review path before a record feels trustworthy."},{"title":"Correction has a home","detail":"The app includes transaction editing and review."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · transaction confidence and review","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person reviewing everyday spending","context":"Using transaction alerts to understand money movement.","need":"Trust the record enough to review and correct it.","friction":"An ambiguous alert can become a plausible but incorrect transaction.","implication":"Make uncertainty visible and correction recoverable."}},"theme":{"layout":"statement","mode":"light","paper":"#f4f6f3","ink":"#161a18","dim":"#59665e","line":"#d4ded6","accent":"#16745f","wash":"#dcefe8","bodyFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","displayFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A record you need to trust","process":"Uncertainty should stay visible","decisions":"Review before confidence","outcome":"The transaction journey","reflection":"Evaluating the mistakes"},"decisionHeading":"Make uncertain transactions easy to review.","resultHeading":"A suggested transaction, open to review."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/premoney-dashboard.webp","presentation":"portrait","alt":"PreMoney actual Android dashboard showing its empty state and insufficient-data scores","caption":"Actual Android dashboard with no transactions. Scores stay unavailable until there is enough information; SMS access was skipped for this capture.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"PreMoney 1.13.0 release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026. No bank messages or personal transactions.","width":1080,"height":1920},"nextStory":{"slug":"soundbox","name":"SoundBox","headline":"The song waits until you are ready."}},"soundbox":{"slug":"soundbox","name":"SoundBox","kind":"product","headline":"The song waits until you are ready.","summary":"A piano-learning app that connects familiar songs, visible keys, and listening-based progression at the player’s pace.","role":"Learning concept, practice flow & feedback design","status":"Working app","context":"Music learning · Working product","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"arrangement levels"},{"value":"Wait mode","label":"default practice rhythm"},{"value":"On-device","label":"audio processing"}],"problem":"Someone who wants to play a familiar song may not yet read notation or keep up with a timed lesson. SoundBox starts from the song and a picture of the keyboard, then waits for the player to produce the expected note or chord.","process":["I organised the learning experience around a song, an approachable arrangement, and a repeatable passage. The default practice mode waits for the learner instead of making the person chase a clock.","Listening feedback needs a clear ready, heard, and retry state. Recognition reliability has not been established across real instruments and rooms, so recovery deserves as much design attention as successful progression."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make progression depend on readiness","choice":"The default mode advances after the expected musical input rather than at a fixed tempo.","reason":"A beginner can concentrate on finding the right movement without constantly falling behind the interface.","tradeoff":"Recognition errors can still interrupt the experience, so recovery and a clear input state are essential."},{"title":"Offer more than one way into a song","choice":"Chord, simplified, and full-melody arrangements support different levels of challenge.","reason":"A learner can begin with something recognisable and increase complexity when ready.","tradeoff":"An automatically simplified arrangement still needs musical review; lower note density is not always easier to play."},{"title":"Support repetition without punishment","choice":"Section looping and restrained progress mechanics accompany local practice history.","reason":"Practice often means repeating one difficult passage, not completing a song from the beginning.","tradeoff":"Streaks and XP can distract from learning if they reward activity without meaningful progress."}],"outcome":"I designed practice around visible keys, listening-based progression and repeatable passages. Different arrangements give learners more than one way into a song. The app lets them stay with a difficult note; recognition reliability and learning outcomes need separate evaluation.","reflection":"I would watch the full practice loop: listening, finding a key, playing it, interpreting the feedback and trying again. A passage-completion count would miss much of what makes that experience frustrating or useful.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: observe a beginner learning one short passage with the default wait mode, including how they recover from missed detections.","Proposed benchmark: test recognised notes and chords under varied room noise, microphones, and keyboards; report false advances separately from missed inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, How the listening works and Practising","detail":"Arrangements, paced learning, recognition approach, local processing and practice loops. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/soundbox","cover":"assets/products/soundbox.svg","coverKind":"title","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":36,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"The song waits [[until you are ready.]]","scene":"Imagine knowing the song you want to play, but being unable to read a page of notation or keep up with a timed lesson. Finding the right key already takes attention.","question":"What changes when the lesson follows the learner’s pace?","insight":"[[Removing the clock changes the interaction]]. The default practice loop waits for the expected note or chord, with space to retry.","pivot":"I connected a visible keyboard, familiar songs, and listening-based progression to make the next musical action understandable.","takeaway":"A learning tool should make another attempt feel possible.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Know which key or chord comes next."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Try it without racing a timer."},{"title":"Listen","detail":"Let the app recognise the input."},{"title":"Repeat","detail":"Loop the difficult passage when useful."}],"decisionLeads":["Let readiness decide the next step.","Offer more than one way into the song.","Support repetition without punishment."],"annotations":[{"title":"A paced practice loop","detail":"The current chord, visible keyboard and Wait for me control make the next attempt clear."},{"title":"Recognition has limits","detail":"Real room and instrument conditions remain evaluation work."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · wait-mode practice","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Beginner practising a song","context":"Repeating a difficult passage at a comfortable pace.","need":"Find the notes and continue when ready.","friction":"A fixed tempo or unclear listening feedback can interrupt practice.","implication":"Wait for the learner and make retry states clear."}},"theme":{"layout":"practice","mode":"dark","paper":"#0f1017","ink":"#f2f3f8","dim":"#b8bfd1","line":"#42495f","accent":"#b8aaff","wash":"#302b4b","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Nunito","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A difficult passage","process":"The learner sets the pace","decisions":"Feedback without a race","outcome":"The practice loop","reflection":"Listening in real conditions"},"decisionHeading":"Let the learner set the pace.","resultHeading":"Stay with the passage. Continue when ready."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/soundbox-practice.png","alt":"Original SoundBox practice screen with chord sequence, keyboard and Wait for me mode.","caption":"The practice screen keeps the current chord, keyboard and pace controls together.","label":"Practice interface","source":"SoundBox/docs/play-screen.png","focus":[{"title":"Recognise","detail":"The current chord and keyboard remain visible."},{"title":"Repeat","detail":"A passage can be approached at the learner’s pace."},{"title":"Control the pace","detail":"Wait for me is an explicit mode."}],"width":1080,"height":2400,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":25,"y":1470,"width":1030,"height":700,"caption":"The keyboard and Wait for me control sit together in the practice screen.","alt":"Detail of the original SoundBox keyboard, listening button and Wait for me practice mode."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"wheelie","name":"Wheelie","headline":"Different starting points. One place to meet."}},"wheelie":{"slug":"wheelie","name":"Wheelie","kind":"product","headline":"Different starting points. One place to meet.","summary":"A group-riding beta that connects rendezvous planning, rider location, helmet voice, and ride summaries.","role":"Service idea, coordination journeys & interaction constraints","status":"Beta · accessibility polish in progress","context":"Group mobility · Working product","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"documented product pillars"},{"value":"64px+","label":"primary target intent"},{"value":"Beta","label":"not a safety validation"}],"problem":"Group riding begins before everyone reaches the same road. People start in different places, need a meeting point, and can lose contact after setting off. Wheelie brings planning, group location, and helmet voice into one service concept, with large, simple controls as an explicit design constraint.","process":["I framed three moments: meeting before a ride, coordinating with the group, and looking back afterwards. Each moment needs a different level of detail and attention.","The constrained physical context informs large targets, restrained choices, and clear connection states. The next evaluation belongs in stationary or controlled settings; the beta is not evidence of safe in-motion interaction."],"decisions":[{"title":"Begin with a rendezvous, not just a destination","choice":"The service computes a meeting point for people starting from different places.","reason":"The group’s coordination problem exists before shared navigation can begin.","tradeoff":"A fair computed point may not be a suitable physical stopping place; people need to review the suggestion."},{"title":"Reduce reliance on precise gestures","choice":"The design calls for large primary targets and simple tap/drag interactions.","reason":"A constrained physical context should affect the interface model from the start.","tradeoff":"Large buttons alone cannot establish safe in-motion use. Evaluation should begin in stationary or controlled settings."},{"title":"Separate coordination from reflection","choice":"Live map and voice support the current group; ride summaries serve the post-ride story.","reason":"Different moments deserve different information and levels of attention.","tradeoff":"Social summaries can create incentives around speed; the content priorities deserve review before wider use."}],"outcome":"I organised the beta around meeting before a ride, coordinating with the group and reviewing the ride afterwards. Meeting points, group location, helmet voice and summaries serve those different moments. Large controls are a design intention, not evidence that the app is safe to operate while moving.","reflection":"I would move as much setup as possible into the time before the ride. The next checks belong in a stationary or controlled setting, with particular attention to connection status and anything that asks the rider to look away.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: test rendezvous planning and headset controls while stationary, then assess attention demands under an appropriately controlled protocol.","Proposed checks: poor connectivity, location permission changes, unavailable voice, battery use, and clarity about whether rider positions are stale."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and design.md","detail":"Product pillars, beta status, target-size intent and unfinished accessibility/glove review. Reviewed 15 October 2026."},{"label":"Interface capture · 31 October 2026","detail":"Wheelie release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/wheelie-start.webp","alt":"Wheelie native Android sign-in screen with blank email and password fields","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. Ride planning and group features are described from the project documentation; no account or rider location was used.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/wheelie","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/wheelie-start.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":39,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Different starting points. [[One place to meet.]]","scene":"Imagine a group starting from different parts of a city. Sharing a destination does not yet answer where to meet or how to stay coordinated once the ride begins.","question":"Which coordination decisions belong before the group is moving?","insight":"[[The ride starts with a rendezvous]]. Planning, rider location, and helmet voice solve different moments of the same service.","pivot":"I connected those moments while treating attention, large controls, and clear connection states as design constraints.","takeaway":"Good coordination should ask for less attention at the busiest moment.","journey":[{"title":"Plan","detail":"Review a suitable meeting point while stopped."},{"title":"Coordinate","detail":"Know who is where and whether the information is current."},{"title":"Reflect","detail":"Tell the ride’s story after it is over."}],"decisionLeads":["Solve the meeting before the navigation.","Reduce the precision an action requires.","Keep the ride and its later story separate."],"annotations":[{"title":"A connected service","detail":"The three pillars cover meeting, communication, and ride summaries."},{"title":"A beta with evaluation ahead","detail":"Accessibility and glove polish remain in progress."}],"evidenceTag":"Beta artifact · controlled evaluation next","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Ride organiser coordinating a group","context":"Preparing meeting points and keeping a group in contact.","need":"Review a rendezvous and understand group connection status.","friction":"Different starting places and physical interaction constraints complicate coordination.","implication":"Prioritise preparation, large targets, and clear connection feedback."}},"theme":{"layout":"rendezvous","mode":"light","paper":"#f7f9ff","ink":"#1d2a41","dim":"#5b6b84","line":"#d2dcee","accent":"#0b57b4","wash":"#e2ecff","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Before the group sets off","process":"Coordination comes first","decisions":"Designing for the context","outcome":"A ride in three moments","reflection":"Controlled evaluation next"},"decisionHeading":"Change the interface around the ride.","resultHeading":"Prepare together. Coordinate clearly. Reflect later."},"visual":{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/wheelie-start.webp","presentation":"portrait","alt":"Wheelie native Android sign-in screen with blank email and password fields","caption":"Actual Android sign-in screen. Ride planning and group features are described from the project documentation; no account or rider location was used.","label":"Actual project interface","source":"Wheelie release APK, captured in an isolated Android emulator, 31 October 2026.","width":1080,"height":1920},"nextStory":{"slug":"matinee","name":"Matinee","headline":"Less scrolling. A film worth saving."}},"matinee":{"slug":"matinee","name":"Matinee","kind":"product","headline":"Less scrolling. A film worth saving.","summary":"A cinema-tracking product connecting discovery, a personal watchlist, ratings, and locally generated recommendations.","role":"Discovery concept, browsing journeys & responsive UX","status":"Interactive app","context":"Entertainment discovery · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"media types: films and series"},{"value":"Local","label":"recommendation scoring"},{"value":"Adaptive","label":"phone and wider layouts"}],"problem":"Finding a title and choosing what to watch are related but different tasks. Matinee connects browsing with a personal record of saved and watched titles, using that history to shape the next set of suggestions.","process":["I connected browsing with the personal record of saved, rated, and watched titles. The design question is how that history can make the next discovery session more relevant without becoming repetitive.","Language preferences, variety, and recognisable film/series labels inform the browsing experience. Navigation and poster density should adapt to the screen while keeping the selected title and next action clear."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep a title’s type visible","choice":"Movie/series identifiers appear in discovery and combined lists.","reason":"A person choosing a two-hour film has a different commitment from someone starting a series.","tradeoff":"A badge helps scanning but does not replace runtime and season information near a decision."},{"title":"Let personal history influence discovery","choice":"Ratings and watch patterns feed a local scoring process, with controls against repetitive franchises or genres.","reason":"Recommendations should reflect preference without becoming a list of near-duplicates.","tradeoff":"A scoring rule is not evidence of relevance. Cold-start and unexpected recommendations need separate evaluation."},{"title":"Adapt the structure to the screen","choice":"Phone navigation becomes a side rail at wider sizes, and poster grids expand.","reason":"The same task can use space differently without turning a larger screen into a stretched phone layout.","tradeoff":"Density should still preserve readable text, meaningful focus order, and a clear selected title."}],"outcome":"I connected discovery, watchlists, history and ratings so previous choices can inform the next one. Film and series context, language preferences and variety all have a place in that flow. I have not established a measured recommendation-accuracy result.","reflection":"I would ask whether people found something they wanted to watch, not just something they clicked. I would also check whether suggestions become too familiar after a few ratings.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: compare a new profile with a rated-history profile and ask participants why each suggestion feels relevant or repetitive.","Proposed measure: useful selections, unwanted repetition, and confidence choosing across preferred languages."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Current mobile architecture, adaptive layout, watchlist rules and local recommendation factors. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://matinee.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/matinee.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":42,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Less scrolling. [[A film worth saving.]]","scene":"Imagine having plenty of saved titles and still spending the evening deciding. Discovery only becomes useful when it helps a person make a choice that fits.","question":"How can personal history help without filling the feed with more of the same?","insight":"[[Preference and variety need to work together]]. Ratings can guide the next suggestions, while repetition and language choices deserve their own controls.","pivot":"I connected watching, rating, and discovery into a loop, with a layout that adapts to how much room the person has.","takeaway":"A recommendation earns its place by helping someone choose.","journey":[{"title":"Save","detail":"Keep the titles that interest you."},{"title":"Watch & rate","detail":"Give your history useful preference signals."},{"title":"Discover","detail":"Bring that context back into the next choice."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the commitment visible.","Let the history influence the next screen.","Use the screen’s space deliberately."],"annotations":[{"title":"Discovery and tracking connect","detail":"The archived capture shows the browsing surface."},{"title":"Relevance is an evaluation question","detail":"Local scoring makes the approach concrete without establishing accuracy."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · history-to-discovery loop","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Viewer deciding what to watch next","context":"Balancing a personal watch history with a desire for something different.","need":"Choose a relevant title without repeatedly seeing near-duplicates.","friction":"A large catalogue creates more browsing without necessarily creating confidence.","implication":"Connect history with language, variety, and title context."}},"theme":{"layout":"cinema","mode":"dark","paper":"#0a0a0c","ink":"#f3f4f6","dim":"#b1b3c0","line":"#3d3541","accent":"#ff8eb5","wash":"#3e2030","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Outfit","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Something worth watching","process":"History without repetition","decisions":"Designing the next choice","outcome":"A personal discovery loop","reflection":"Finding relevance in practice"},"decisionHeading":"Help someone choose what to watch.","resultHeading":"A viewing history that leads somewhere new."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/matinee.webp","alt":"Matinee — archived product interface","caption":"Original Matinee discovery screen. Posters and titles provide the starting point for browsing films and series.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"birthsky","name":"BirthSky","headline":"Turn a time and place into a sky to keep."}},"birthsky":{"slug":"birthsky","name":"BirthSky","kind":"product","headline":"Turn a time and place into a sky to keep.","summary":"An astronomy-inspired experience that turns a time and place into a customisable star-map wallpaper.","role":"Experience concept, input journey & visual direction","status":"Working browser project","context":"Personalised visualisation · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Time + place","label":"core input"},{"value":"3","label":"documented visual modes"},{"value":"2","label":"wallpaper formats"}],"problem":"A star map can be a technical chart or a personal object. BirthSky connects a simple biographical prompt to a visual reveal, then gives the person control over labels and the final wallpaper format.","process":["I organised the experience into a familiar prompt, a personal reveal, optional exploration, and a keepsake. Name, date, time, and place give the abstract sky a recognisable starting point.","The reveal needs understandable inputs. Unknown birth times, time zones, and any collection of personal details deserve clear explanations before a precise-looking image encourages confidence."],"decisions":[{"title":"Begin with a recognisable personal question","choice":"A short form collects the time and location before revealing the sky.","reason":"The input makes an otherwise abstract astronomical chart meaningful to the person requesting it.","tradeoff":"Unknown birth times and historical time zones require careful explanation; a precise-looking map can still rest on uncertain inputs."},{"title":"Make annotation a choice","choice":"The result supports alternative modes and toggles for constellation lines and names.","reason":"A person may want to learn from the sky or simply use it as a visual keepsake.","tradeoff":"Switches need clear effects without making the result feel like a complex scientific console."},{"title":"Design the output as part of the journey","choice":"Phone and desktop wallpaper downloads follow the reveal.","reason":"A useful result should fit where the person intends to use it.","tradeoff":"Export must preserve legibility and composition across aspect ratios rather than just scale one screenshot."}],"outcome":"I designed the journey from entering a time and place to adjusting a star map and exporting a wallpaper. Annotation and output choices let the person decide what belongs in the final image. Time uncertainty and data handling need to stay clear throughout.","reflection":"I would make it easier to work with an approximate birth time before adding another visual style. The result is personal, so the person should understand which inputs shaped it and what those inputs can reasonably tell them.","nextSteps":["Proposed validation: compare known dates and locations with an authoritative sky reference, including historical daylight-saving and time-zone cases.","Proposed iteration: explain any logging before submission and make uncertain birth-time choices visible in the final result."],"sources":[{"label":"StarMap/index.html and js/app.js","detail":"Input flow, result controls, modes, export entry points and configured logging integration."},{"label":"StarMap/js/astronomy.js and js/exporter.js","detail":"Coordinate calculations and wallpaper generation. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://birthsky.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/birthsky.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":45,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Turn a time and place [[into a sky to keep.]]","scene":"Imagine choosing a meaningful date and place, then seeing the sky interpreted as something personal. The experience has to connect a precise-looking visual to understandable inputs.","question":"How can a technical chart become a meaningful personal object?","insight":"[[The reveal and the keepsake are one journey]]. Time and place shape the view; annotation and aspect ratio shape how it is kept.","pivot":"I connected the personal prompt to a customisable sky and wallpaper outputs.","takeaway":"Make the inputs understandable enough to trust the reveal.","journey":[{"title":"Choose","detail":"Enter the time and location."},{"title":"Reveal","detail":"See the sky rendered from those inputs."},{"title":"Make it yours","detail":"Choose labels and a visual mode."},{"title":"Keep","detail":"Export for a phone or desktop."}],"decisionLeads":["Begin with a personal question.","Let annotation become a choice.","Design the output as part of the experience."],"annotations":[{"title":"A personal opening","detail":"The original product connects a biographical prompt to a sky view."},{"title":"A result beyond the page","detail":"Wallpaper exports give the reveal a place to live."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · input, render, export","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person creating a personal sky keepsake","context":"Entering a meaningful date and place, then choosing a wallpaper.","need":"Understand the inputs and keep a result that feels personal.","friction":"Uncertain times can produce a precise-looking picture.","implication":"Explain uncertainty and make visual annotation optional."}},"theme":{"layout":"constellation","mode":"dark","paper":"#030311","ink":"#e8e6f0","dim":"#b5b1ca","line":"#48415d","accent":"#d9bc73","wash":"#31291b","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Cormorant Garamond","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A moment that matters","process":"The reveal starts with the input","decisions":"Designing the keepsake","outcome":"A sky you can keep","reflection":"What an unknown time changes"},"decisionHeading":"Make the date, place and reveal understandable.","resultHeading":"A personal moment, made into a keepsake."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/birthsky.webp","alt":"BirthSky — archived product interface","caption":"Original BirthSky interface for entering a birth date, time and place to explore the sky.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"scalesee","name":"ScaleSee","headline":"How much bigger? Show the difference."}},"scalesee":{"slug":"scalesee","name":"ScaleSee","kind":"product","headline":"How much bigger? Show the difference.","summary":"A visual comparison tool that turns quantities into scenes while keeping the meaning of scale explicit.","role":"Visual explanation, comparison journeys & interaction design","status":"Working browser product","context":"Quantitative explanation · Working product","facts":[{"value":"11","label":"categories listed in the source"},{"value":"2","label":"scale modes"},{"value":"Shareable","label":"comparison URLs"}],"problem":"A ratio can be mathematically clear and still be difficult to picture. ScaleSee turns two quantities into a shared visual comparison, choosing a representation that fits the kind of quantity instead of using the same chart for everything.","process":["I selected a visual reference appropriate to each kind of quantity: a ground line for height, nesting for land, or a shared course for speed. The reference is part of the explanation.","An extreme ratio creates a useful design tension: showing true scale can make one quantity nearly disappear. A compressed alternative needs an explicit label that remains with a shared comparison or exported image."],"decisions":[{"title":"Choose the picture to fit the quantity","choice":"Height shares a ground line, land can be nested, and speed uses a common course.","reason":"A meaningful reference can make a ratio easier to interpret than a generic bar.","tradeoff":"Every visual metaphor introduces assumptions; those assumptions need to be stated where they affect the comparison."},{"title":"Keep scale transformations explicit","choice":"True scale and logarithmic scale are separate modes.","reason":"When a small quantity almost disappears, that can be the honest answer; a compressed alternative should declare the change.","tradeoff":"A log view can make a scene more legible while reducing intuitive proportionality."},{"title":"Let the comparison leave the tool","choice":"A URL carries the selected comparison and an image export preserves the current drawing.","reason":"An explanation is more useful when someone can bring it into the conversation that prompted it.","tradeoff":"An exported image needs labels and scale context because it no longer has interactive controls beside it."}],"outcome":"I matched comparison scenes to the quantity being explored and kept the scale mode explicit. Sharing and image export let a comparison leave the tool with its context. Extreme ratios still need testing for correct interpretation.","reflection":"Sometimes a proportional picture is too small to read and an adjusted view is easy to misread. I would check whether someone can identify the scale mode and explain what the picture does to the original numbers.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask readers to estimate the ratio before and after each view, including an extreme comparison and a logarithmic one.","Proposed check: confirm labels, units, assumptions, and scale mode survive in every shared or exported artifact."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What’s in it and Notes on the numbers","detail":"Category inventory, visual metaphors, scale-mode distinction and sharing/export. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://scalesee.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/scalesee.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":48,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"How much bigger? [[Show the difference.]]","scene":"Imagine comparing a million with a billion. The arithmetic is simple; making the difference feel concrete is another task.","question":"When does making both quantities visible change the meaning of the picture?","insight":"[[Legibility and proportional truth can pull apart]]. True scale may make one quantity almost disappear; a logarithmic view needs to declare its transformation.","pivot":"I used category-specific scenes and explicit scale modes, with enough context to travel in a shared image.","takeaway":"An explanation should keep its assumptions visible when it leaves the tool.","journey":[{"title":"Compare","detail":"Choose quantities of the same kind."},{"title":"See","detail":"Use a scene that fits the quantity."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"Understand the selected scale mode."},{"title":"Share","detail":"Carry the labels and context into the output."}],"decisionLeads":["Let the quantity choose the picture.","Make a transformed scale explicit.","Keep the explanation intact when shared."],"annotations":[{"title":"A scene for the quantity","detail":"The product gives a ratio a concrete visual reference."},{"title":"Context travels with it","detail":"Scale, units, and assumptions matter in an exported comparison."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · comparison and export","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person trying to explain a large difference","context":"Comparing two quantities for a conversation or illustration.","need":"Understand the ratio and share it without losing context.","friction":"Extreme differences make small values disappear or invite misleading compression.","implication":"Declare scale changes and preserve labels in exports."}},"theme":{"layout":"comparison","mode":"light","paper":"#f2f1f0","ink":"#19181c","dim":"#625f68","line":"#d3d0d7","accent":"#8b3fa8","wash":"#eddff2","bodyFont":"Inter Tight","displayFont":"Inter Tight","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A difference hard to picture","process":"The reference does the explaining","decisions":"Designing an honest comparison","outcome":"The ratio, made visible","reflection":"Checking what the reader sees"},"decisionHeading":"Choose a reference people can understand.","resultHeading":"Two quantities. One understandable relationship."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/scalesee.webp","alt":"ScaleSee — archived product interface","caption":"Original ScaleSee comparison surface, with two quantities drawn against the same scale.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"where-would-i-be","name":"Where Would I Be…?","headline":"Where would this place be as the plates move?"}},"where-would-i-be":{"slug":"where-would-i-be","name":"Where Would I Be…?","kind":"product","headline":"Where would this place be as the plates move?","summary":"An interactive globe that lets a chosen place move with a plate-motion model and makes the model’s limits part of the story.","role":"Experience framing, interaction sequence & spatial explanation","status":"Working browser product","context":"Scientific storytelling · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"place-led starting question"},{"value":"Progressive","label":"details on demand"},{"value":"Modelled","label":"extrapolation, not prediction"}],"problem":"Plate motion is slow enough to be invisible in everyday life and large enough to reshape a world over time. This project gives that scale a personal anchor: choose a place, move the clock, and follow the ground beneath it.","process":["I gave the opening one task: choose a place. A time control and a short result then introduce motion, with optional detail kept out of the main globe.","The visual story needs to carry its qualification with it. Constant-motion extrapolation can invite exploration, but the result should not read as a reconstruction or prediction of the continents."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give each stage one main task","choice":"Choose a place first, then explore time, with optional comparison later.","reason":"A clear sequence can make a complex subject approachable without presenting every control at once.","tradeoff":"Progressive disclosure should not hide the model’s most important qualification until after someone has formed a false impression."},{"title":"Protect the space where the answer is drawn","choice":"The design keeps the main globe clear and groups controls in a bottom dock.","reason":"The selected point and its path are the explanation; a floating panel over them would compete with the result.","tradeoff":"A dock must adapt to available height so it does not consume the same space on a small screen."},{"title":"Make the scientific boundary legible","choice":"The source describes plate movement as a constant-motion extrapolation with increasing limits over long horizons.","reason":"Personal storytelling can encourage exploration without claiming to know the future shape of continents.","tradeoff":"A smooth animation can still imply certainty, so the qualification belongs near the time result."}],"outcome":"I used a chosen location as the starting point for exploring time on the globe. Time controls, comparison and optional layers support the exploration, while the model limit stays near the result. The long-term path is an extrapolation, not a geological forecast.","reflection":"I would ask someone to explain why the point moves and why the long-term path is conditional. The interaction should leave them with a useful question about plate motion, not misplaced confidence in a prediction.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a visitor to describe what changed and what assumptions the model makes after a short exploration.","Proposed checks: narrow-screen globe visibility, reduced-motion behaviour, keyboard interaction, and whether uncertainty stays visible at extreme dates."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, The layout rule and How much to trust it","detail":"Interaction sequence, unobstructed visual space and constant-motion model limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://wherewouldibe.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/wherewouldibe.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":51,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Where would this place be [[as the plates move?]]","scene":"Imagine following the patch of crust beneath a familiar place over a time span you cannot experience. One personal point of reference gives deep time somewhere to begin.","question":"How can a compelling journey keep the model’s limits in view?","insight":"[[A smooth animation is still conditional]]. Holding present plate velocities constant creates an extrapolation whose limits grow with the horizon.","pivot":"I kept the globe clear, began with a place, and let the time control reveal a concise answer before offering deeper detail.","takeaway":"Let the story invite exploration without turning a model into a prediction.","journey":[{"title":"Anchor","detail":"Start from one familiar location."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Move the time control and follow its path."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"Read the result alongside the model’s assumptions."}],"decisionLeads":["Give the first screen one useful question.","Protect the space where the answer is drawn.","Keep the scientific boundary near the result."],"annotations":[{"title":"A personal anchor","detail":"The selected point connects a large timescale to a familiar location."},{"title":"Details on demand","detail":"The interface leaves the globe room to explain the path."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · model and interaction sequence","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Curious visitor exploring deep time","context":"Using a familiar location to make plate movement tangible.","need":"Follow a place through time and understand what the model means.","friction":"A smooth moving globe can look more certain than an extrapolation is.","implication":"Keep the model qualification near the time result."}},"theme":{"layout":"deep-time","mode":"dark","paper":"#05050c","ink":"#efedf8","dim":"#b4b0cb","line":"#48435d","accent":"#3ddbff","wash":"#152f3b","bodyFont":"Space Grotesk","displayFont":"Bricolage Grotesque","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A familiar place, another time","process":"A personal anchor for deep time","decisions":"Designing the time journey","outcome":"The moving point","reflection":"Where the model stops"},"decisionHeading":"Keep each prediction’s limits visible.","resultHeading":"A point through time, with the model in view."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/wherewouldibe.webp","alt":"Where Would I Be…? — archived product interface","caption":"Original location entry screen. Choosing a place starts the exploration of plate motion through time.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"the-blog","name":"The Blog","headline":"A home for my writing, in the form it needs."}},"the-blog":{"slug":"the-blog","name":"The Blog","kind":"product","headline":"A home for my writing, in the form it needs.","summary":"An editorial site with a browser-based publishing workflow for poems, essays, fiction, and serial writing.","role":"Editorial structure, reading experience & author workflow","status":"Working publishing system","context":"Publishing experience · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"reader and author workflows"},{"value":"Drafts","label":"explicit publishing control"},{"value":"Verse","label":"preserved line breaks"}],"problem":"A personal writing practice should not require a technical deployment ritual for every post. The Blog connects a reading experience with a browser editor that handles content, images, drafts, and publishing through a familiar form.","process":["I framed writing, reviewing, and publishing as separate commitments. The editor should support the author’s work without requiring technical knowledge of how a post reaches the website.","The reading experience follows the form of the writing: poetry keeps its line breaks, while topics and series help a reader continue. Publication feedback should make draft and live states easy to distinguish."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give publishing a clear state","choice":"Draft and published are separate states, with a deliberate release action.","reason":"Writing and releasing a piece are different commitments and should remain distinguishable.","tradeoff":"Publishing can take time. The author needs clear confirmation of what is saved and what is live."},{"title":"Let the content form affect the layout","choice":"Poetry preserves line breaks and an appropriate verse treatment, while ordinary articles retain a reading layout.","reason":"The way a piece is written can be part of its meaning.","tradeoff":"Formatting conventions must be discoverable in the editor rather than depend on hidden technical knowledge."},{"title":"Make ongoing writing navigable","choice":"Topics and series connect related pieces within the editorial structure.","reason":"A reader should be able to follow a thread without knowing the order it was published.","tradeoff":"The documented title-based series matching is convenient but implicit; a visible series field would make mistakes easier to avoid."}],"outcome":"I gave poems, essays and ongoing stories a reading structure suited to each form. The publishing flow separates drafts from release, while topics and series help a reader continue. Poetry keeps its line breaks instead of being squeezed into an ordinary article template.","reflection":"I would make draft status and series membership more explicit in the editor. Those small details affect whether I can publish the right version and whether someone can find the next part of a story.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: publish a draft, update an existing post, and add a series instalment without using development tools.","Proposed measure: accidental publication, broken links after slug edits, and clarity about whether the latest save is live."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, Writing a post and Things worth knowing","detail":"Browser editor, draft/build publishing path, verse formatting and title-based series conventions. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://blogs.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/blogs.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":54,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"A home for my writing, [[in the form it needs.]]","scene":"Imagine finishing a poem and then having to switch from writing into a development workflow. The mechanics of publishing can become a reason to put the piece aside.","question":"What should the author have to think about after the writing is done?","insight":"[[Publishing state belongs in the editor]]. A draft, a save, and a live page are different moments that need clear feedback.","pivot":"I connected a browser editor with a content-aware reading experience, respecting verse and serial writing along the way.","takeaway":"The tool should respect the shape of the writing.","journey":[{"title":"Compose","detail":"Work through an editorial interface."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Keep draft status and content choices visible."},{"title":"Release","detail":"Carry the saved piece through the build into the reading experience."}],"decisionLeads":["Make publication a visible commitment.","Let the form of the writing matter.","Make the next piece easy to find."],"annotations":[{"title":"An editorial front door","detail":"The site introduces the writing in its own visual voice."},{"title":"A workflow behind it","detail":"The browser editor supports the ongoing publishing practice."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · editorial workflow","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Writer publishing & reader continuing a piece","context":"An author moves from draft to release; a reader moves between related writing.","need":"Preserve the form of the writing and a clear route to the next piece.","friction":"Publication states and implicit series conventions can be difficult to understand.","implication":"Give drafts, verse, and series explicit structure."}},"theme":{"layout":"editorial","mode":"light","paper":"#faf8f3","ink":"#14130f","dim":"#5a564c","line":"#d7d0c0","accent":"#6b3fa0","wash":"#eee5f5","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A draft is not a release","process":"The form is part of the writing","decisions":"Designing reader and author journeys","outcome":"Writing with a place to live","reflection":"Making publication legible"},"decisionHeading":"Make it easy to find and continue reading.","resultHeading":"A home for drafts, poems, and continuing stories."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/blogs.webp","alt":"The Blog — archived product interface","caption":"Original writing homepage under the name अvinash, with poems, essays, fiction and a written podcast.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"office-games","name":"The Office Games","headline":"Get everyone in. Keep the round moving."}},"office-games":{"slug":"office-games","name":"The Office Games","kind":"product","headline":"Get everyone in. Keep the round moving.","summary":"A team-trivia experience exploring quick room entry, visible roles, and shared game state for a short social break.","role":"Social game concept, room journey & turn-taking UX","status":"Working project · trivia module","context":"Social play · Working product","facts":[{"value":"4","label":"letters in a room code"},{"value":"Team-based","label":"turn and score model"},{"value":"Explicit","label":"local or network mode"}],"problem":"A short team game loses momentum when people are unsure how to join or whose turn it is. The reviewed Office Games module centres on a simple room flow, a lobby, team roles, and a visible answering/stealing sequence.","process":["I mapped the reviewed trivia journey from joining a room to understanding teams, turns, answering, and stealing. The broader games catalogue is outside the evidence available for this case study.","The UX needs to explain the connection mode before a group commits to a game. Trying a local demonstration and joining colleagues on separate devices are different experiences."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make joining a small action","choice":"A four-letter room code and a player name provide a concise entry path.","reason":"A social break should spend more of its time playing than explaining setup.","tradeoff":"A short code still needs clear invalid-room and full-room handling, and does not establish secure identity."},{"title":"Expose responsibility in the game state","choice":"The interface distinguishes answering and stealing teams, active players, and scores.","reason":"People need to see who can act and why the round is waiting.","tradeoff":"Role-based interaction becomes confusing if waiting states or connection loss are not explained."},{"title":"Keep connection mode understandable","choice":"The setup distinguishes a local demonstration from connected play.","reason":"A demo can help someone try the game, but should not promise multi-device play it cannot provide.","tradeoff":"A connected game needs clear waiting, conflict, reconnection, and host-recovery states."}],"outcome":"For the trivia flow documented here, I focused on room entry, teams, scores and the answering-and-stealing sequence. The interface gives the group a shared view of who can act next. Local demo mode and connected play need different expectations.","reflection":"I would run a short session and pay attention to the pauses: joining, waiting for a team, recovering a connection and changing the host. The answer screen is only one part of whether the break feels easy to join.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: run a short trivia session with several participants and record setup explanations, confused turns, and waiting time.","Proposed checks: separate-device joining, reconnection, duplicate actions, host departure, and a clearly labelled local demo mode."],"sources":[{"label":"src/App.jsx and src/gameService.js","detail":"Reviewed room/lobby/trivia journey, short codes, team state, local-storage mock service and Firebase path. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://officegames.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/officegames.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":57,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Get everyone in. [[Keep the round moving.]]","scene":"Imagine a short team break being spent explaining how to join, who can answer, and why the game is waiting. The social rhythm depends on those transitions being obvious.","question":"What does the group need to understand at every handoff?","insight":"[[Shared state has to become shared understanding]]. Room entry, team roles, and the answering/stealing sequence all contribute to the pace.","pivot":"I focused the reviewed trivia module on a concise joining path and visible responsibility through each round.","takeaway":"A social game moves when everyone knows what happens next.","journey":[{"title":"Join","detail":"Use a short code and a player name."},{"title":"Orient","detail":"See the teams and who can act."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Follow the answering and stealing sequence."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Keep scores and waiting states understandable."}],"decisionLeads":["Make joining a small action.","Show why a round is waiting.","Keep the connection promise clear."],"annotations":[{"title":"A social entry point","detail":"The product offers a quick route into a shared game."},{"title":"Reviewed scope: trivia","detail":"Local demo and network-backed play make different connection promises."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · reviewed trivia module","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Colleague joining a short group game","context":"Entering a room and learning a team role during an office break.","need":"Join quickly and know whose turn it is.","friction":"Waiting and role changes become confusing when the interface is silent.","implication":"Make room, role, and next action visible."}},"theme":{"layout":"playroom","mode":"dark","paper":"#17181c","ink":"#f8f9fa","dim":"#bdc2ce","line":"#495160","accent":"#ff94b7","wash":"#422336","bodyFont":"Outfit","displayFont":"Fredoka","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A short break to play","process":"Someone needs the next turn","decisions":"Designing the social rhythm","outcome":"Inside the trivia round","reflection":"What waiting feels like"},"decisionHeading":"Make the next turn clear to everyone.","resultHeading":"A room with a shared social rhythm."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/officegames.webp","alt":"The Office Games — archived product interface","caption":"Original Office Games entry screen. A group chooses a game before joining a shared room.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"forma","name":"Forma","headline":"Health readings with the context attached."}},"forma":{"slug":"forma","name":"Forma","kind":"product","headline":"Health readings with the context attached.","summary":"A native Android health app that brings activity, sleep and personal trends together while making sources, missing data and experimental estimates visible.","role":"Product concept, health-data journeys & interaction design","status":"Native Android app · experimental wellness estimates","context":"Personal health","facts":[{"value":"5","label":"main destinations"},{"value":"44","label":"native metric views"},{"value":"Recorded","label":"measurement times retained"}],"problem":"A watch can collect more measurements than a person can comfortably interpret. A number also changes meaning when it is old, incomplete, imported or estimated. I framed Forma around a daily overview that stays useful without making uncertain data look more reliable than it is.","process":["I organised the experience around five destinations: Today for a daily overview, Sleep for the recorded night, Move for activity and workouts, Trends for comparison, and Health for the measurement library. The native app is the product; the React version in the repository is a design preview.","The data constraints shaped the UX. Samsung Health must share records through Health Connect, permissions can be partial, and not every watch measurement is available. Connection setup therefore needs to explain availability rather than treat an empty reading as a failure or a zero.","The later function audit separated Devices from Your data, moved optional personal tools into settings, removed controls without a working purpose, and put formulas behind expandable details. The archived screens illustrate the native visual direction; the audit and current source establish the later navigation and scope. This is an artifact-based retrospective, without a participant study or clinical validation."],"decisions":[{"title":"Distinguish recorded, missing and estimated values","choice":"Keep source labels and measurement times attached to readings. Preserve gaps in histories, identify sample data, and distinguish sleeping heart rate from a recorded resting-heart-rate value.","reason":"A recent refresh does not mean a sensor reading is recent. People need to understand what a value represents before comparing it with yesterday or using it to interpret their day.","tradeoff":"These labels add density. The hierarchy has to keep essential provenance visible while leaving longer explanations available on demand."},{"title":"Let a score open into its explanation","choice":"Show the score and its contributing values first, then offer the calculation and diagnostics in an expandable sheet. Recovery requires recorded sleep and a usable baseline vital; missing inputs can leave the estimate unavailable.","reason":"An unexplained score can look authoritative. Showing its inputs makes the estimate inspectable and lets the interface explain why the same score can accompany different activity records.","tradeoff":"Transparency does not validate the model. Sleep, recovery and body-energy estimates remain experimental wellness interpretations, distinct from Samsung proprietary scores and medical assessment."},{"title":"Separate connecting a device from managing data","choice":"Devices leads with connection state and the next available action. Your data contains coverage, export, optional import and local reset. Targets and personal tools have their own settings paths.","reason":"Someone trying to connect a watch should not have to interpret a coverage report or navigate file-management controls before granting access.","tradeoff":"Separating the tasks adds destinations. Clear names and back paths matter, particularly when permissions are partial or a watch has not synced."}],"outcome":"I organised the native app around a daily overview, dedicated sleep and movement views, histories and a searchable health library. A score opens into its contributors; device setup and data management have separate destinations. The estimates remain experimental, and Samsung sharing depends on the device and permissions.","reflection":"Separating Devices from Your data made the next action clearer. I would now check whether someone can explain an unavailable or older reading as readily as a complete one, and whether they can find the inputs behind a score.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask people to distinguish sample, shared, direct-watch, older and unavailable readings, then explain what an experimental recovery score does and does not mean.","Proposed device checks: follow granted, denied and partial-permission journeys on a Samsung phone and watch, including delayed sync, expired direct readings and corrected history.","Proposed measures: connection-task completion, mistaken interpretations of missing data, and success finding a score explanation or exporting records."],"sources":[{"label":"Forma README and native interface source","detail":"Reviewed 16 October 2026. Native Android scope, five destinations, Samsung Health/Health Connect integration, measurement timestamps and experimental score limits. The web project is a design preview."},{"label":"docs/DESIGN.md and docs/UX-AUDIT.md","detail":"The later 1.5 audit supersedes the older 46-measurement design note: 44 native metric views, distinct Devices/Your data flows, optional personal tools and expandable score explanations."},{"label":"Archived native captures · sample data","detail":"Today rings and vitals captures from the project output illustrate the native visual system. The Today capture predates the Health tab rename and personal-tool cleanup; the later Your data capture shows the separated data-management flow. Values are demonstration data."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-today.webp","alt":"Archived Forma native Today screen with demo activity rings and sample heart rate","caption":"Archived native Today screen with clearly marked demo values. This capture predates the later Health-tab and personal-tool cleanup.","width":1080,"height":2400},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-data.webp","alt":"Forma Your data screen showing sample coverage, export and separate data controls","caption":"The later Your data panel groups coverage and file controls separately from device setup. All values shown are sample data.","width":1080,"height":2400}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/forma/releases/latest","productActionLabel":"Get Forma for Android","cover":"assets/products/forma.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":6,"productHeaderLabel":"Android release","pdfPage":60,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Health readings [[with the context attached.]]","scene":"Imagine opening a health dashboard after a watch sync. One measurement is recent, one was last recorded days ago, and another has not been shared. Similar-looking numbers can hide very different situations.","question":"How can a daily overview make the meaning of each reading clear?","insight":"[[Data quality is part of the experience]]. Source, measurement time, missing inputs and model coverage change how a reading should be interpreted.","pivot":"I focused the daily overview on readable measurements, with a clear route into history, source context and score explanations.","takeaway":"Clarity includes knowing what the app cannot tell you.","journey":[{"title":"Orient","detail":"See Today with explicit sample or source context."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Open sleep, movement or a measurement history."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"Read the contributors behind an experimental score."},{"title":"Manage","detail":"Use Devices to connect and Your data to inspect coverage or export."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep provenance beside the reading.","Show the inputs behind the score.","Give connection and data tasks separate homes."],"annotations":[{"title":"Native interface","detail":"The Android app uses native screens and charts; the web version is a design preview."},{"title":"Inspectable estimates","detail":"Experimental wellness scores retain input and coverage limits."}],"evidenceTag":"Native artifacts and documented UX audit · sample data in captures","tone":"lime","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android user reviewing Samsung-shared health records","context":"Moving between a daily summary, a specific measurement and a watch connection that may have partial permissions.","need":"Understand the reading and its source before comparing it with a personal pattern.","friction":"Missing, older and estimated values can look like equally reliable facts.","implication":"Keep time, source and availability visible, with deeper explanation one step away."}},"theme":{"layout":"health-record","mode":"dark","paper":"#101211","ink":"#f4f5ec","dim":"#b4c0b5","line":"#414c43","accent":"#d8fc70","wash":"#303d1e","bodyFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","displayFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A reading without its context","process":"Treat availability as part of the design","decisions":"From summary to explanation","outcome":"A native overview with inspectable detail","reflection":"An honest limit is useful information"},"decisionHeading":"Keep the context close to the reading.","resultHeading":"A daily overview with a route to the evidence."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-today.webp","alt":"Archived Forma native Today screen with demo activity rings and sample heart rate","caption":"Archived native Today screen with clearly marked demo values. This capture predates the later Health-tab and personal-tool cleanup.","label":"Archived native interface · sample data","source":"Health++ output/forma-today-rings.png, captured in the project on 13 October 2026. Lossy WebP delivery copy, no UI alteration.","width":1080,"height":2400,"focus":[{"title":"Native interface","detail":"The Android app uses native screens and charts; the web version is a design preview."},{"title":"Inspectable estimates","detail":"Experimental wellness scores retain input and coverage limits."}],"details":[{"decision":0,"x":50,"y":1710,"width":980,"height":430,"alt":"Forma sample heart-rate reading with explicit sample and recorded-range labels","caption":"A detail of the archived Today capture. Sample labels and measurement context stay beside the value."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"ohcaptain","name":"OhCaptain","headline":"Sail your ship. Coordinate the fleet."}},"ohcaptain":{"slug":"ohcaptain","name":"OhCaptain","kind":"product","headline":"Sail your ship. Coordinate the fleet.","summary":"A 3-v-3 naval game exploring shared command, role-specific decisions and a combat interface that keeps the sea in view.","role":"Game concept, captain/operator journeys & tactical interface design","status":"Playable browser prototype · keyboard & mouse","context":"Naval capture-and-hold · Operation Blackwater","facts":[{"value":"3 v 3","label":"captain and two operators per crew"},{"value":"8","label":"capture-and-hold relays"},{"value":"10 min","label":"maximum match window"}],"problem":"In OhCaptain, sailing and firing are only part of the job. Each crew shares supplies, but its captain decides how to spend them. Operators need to request help while both roles keep track of a changing battle. I framed the interface around the next decision a player can actually make.","process":["I separated the crew journey into choosing a station, entering solo play or assembling a room, deploying, and managing the battle. A captain still sails and fights; the extra responsibility is distributing shared supplies. Operators request upgrades or repairs rather than spending the treasury themselves.","The documented Clear Deck revision gives combat a compact score/clock, fleet-alive counts, hull status and a persistent map. Spending moves into the Fleet drawer, while the expanded overview holds rosters, recent events and relay details. Warnings compete for a small number of attention slots instead of becoming a wall of banners.","I reviewed the local playable build, documentation and awareness rules. Solo play with AI fill and peer-to-peer rooms are distinct product paths. Online scope depends on a host keeping the page open; the prototype has no dedicated match service or host migration. Rule checks and seeded scenarios do not establish balance or usability with human crews."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make responsibility visible before deployment","choice":"Offer Captain and Vessel Operator as distinct stations. Captains distribute shared supplies through the Fleet drawer; operators use the same crew context to request an upgrade or repair.","reason":"A shared resource needs a clear decision owner. Role-specific actions let people understand why they can request something but cannot buy it directly.","tradeoff":"The captain can become a bottleneck while also sailing and fighting. Pending requests need to remain discoverable without forcing a panel over combat."},{"title":"Keep essential battle state on the field","choice":"Keep the score, clock, relay balance, fleet-alive counts, hull and corner map available. Put spending in a drawer and detailed rosters, events and defenses in the expanded overview.","reason":"The map helps connect local action to the relay objective. Separating routine awareness from deliberate inspection leaves room to aim and navigate.","tradeoff":"An overview can interrupt action. Solo overview pauses the match; online play continues, so players need to understand that distinction. The prototype targets keyboard and mouse rather than claiming a touch-control experience."},{"title":"Give urgent feedback a limited attention budget","choice":"Show at most one off-screen enemy bearing and reserve one text cue for critical hull, safe healing or an endangered home relay. Limit outgoing damage feedback to two numbers and keep routine radio outside combat.","reason":"An alert is useful only if it helps a player choose an action. A priority order makes a received hit or low hull easier to notice among effects, ships and objectives.","tradeoff":"A quieter interface can hide context that an experienced player wants. The map and overview must provide a dependable route to that detail, and the priority policy needs human playtesting."}],"outcome":"I separated immediate battle awareness, fleet spending and tactical inspection across the combat view, Fleet drawer and overview. The prototype supports solo AI-filled matches and crew rooms in one capture-and-hold arena. The captures here show the actual game; the illustrated harbor is menu artwork.","reflection":"I would put a new crew through requesting a repair, spending supplies and choosing a relay without coaching. Missed requests and time spent searching the overview would help me decide what to simplify next.","nextSteps":["Proposed playtest: form two crews and ask players to explain their role, request or buy a repair, and identify the relay that matters next without coaching.","Proposed checks: invitation and room entry, role reassignment, a guest disconnecting, restrictive peer-to-peer networks, and an honest explanation when a host leaves.","Proposed observations: missed requests, map-to-world confusion, unnoticed critical alerts and time spent in the overview. Compare these before changing the attention policy."],"sources":[{"label":"OhCaptain README · Play, online limits and Clear Deck interface","detail":"Reviewed 16 October 2026. Captain/operator responsibilities, AI fill, peer-to-peer rooms, eight-relay objectives, ten-minute match window and host/network limitations."},{"label":"src/awareness.js, src/main.js and src/combat.css","detail":"Battle interpretation, role-specific controls, attention priority, local damage feedback and the single off-screen enemy-bearing policy."},{"label":"Local playable build and docs/BLACKWATER-ART.md","detail":"Actual solo combat and expanded-map captures from 16 October 2026. The harbor capture includes the project’s generated cinematic key art; it is menu presentation, distinct from the procedural playable arena."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain actual solo gameplay with compact battle score, hull status and persistent map","caption":"Actual local gameplay with an AI-filled crew: compact battle status, a persistent map, the Fleet control and the player’s hull.","width":1280,"height":720},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-map.webp","alt":"OhCaptain expanded tactical map with both fleets, islands and eight labeled relays","caption":"A detail of the actual expanded tactical map. Both fronts, ship positions and relay ownership share the same view; the solo overview pauses play.","width":643,"height":414}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/OhCaptain","productActionLabel":"View the game on GitHub","cover":"assets/products/ohcaptain.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":6,"productHeaderLabel":"GitHub project","pdfPage":63,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Sail your ship. [[Coordinate the fleet.]]","scene":"Imagine taking damage while an operator asks for repairs and an enemy crew captures a relay. The captain must decide where shared supplies go while still steering a ship.","question":"What needs to stay visible when sailing, fighting and command happen together?","insight":"[[Information needs a place and a priority]]. An urgent hull warning, a spending decision and a full fleet overview should not demand attention in the same way.","pivot":"I separated immediate combat awareness, role-specific fleet controls and deliberate tactical inspection.","takeaway":"A crew can coordinate when the next action has an owner.","journey":[{"title":"Choose a station","detail":"Understand captain spending or operator requests."},{"title":"Deploy","detail":"Start with AI fill or assemble a crew through a room."},{"title":"Read the battle","detail":"Keep hull, fleet status and the relay map available."},{"title":"Coordinate","detail":"Open Fleet to spend or request; expand the overview to plan."}],"decisionLeads":["Assign responsibility before the battle.","Give each information layer a place.","Let urgent signals take precedence."],"annotations":[{"title":"Keyboard and mouse","detail":"The playable prototype is designed around desktop sailing, aiming and command controls."},{"title":"Peer-to-peer scope","detail":"Rooms rely on a host and direct peer connections; solo AI-filled play remains a separate path."}],"evidenceTag":"Playable prototype and documented Clear Deck revision · solo captures","tone":"navy","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Player joining a small naval crew","context":"Learning a captain or operator role while coordinating around a finite relay-control match.","need":"Know who can act, what is urgent and where the next objective is.","friction":"Combat, spending requests and tactical detail can overwhelm the same screen.","implication":"Preserve immediate awareness and reveal deliberate command detail when requested."}},"theme":{"layout":"fleet-command","mode":"dark","paper":"#0b141b","ink":"#e9edf0","dim":"#afc1cc","line":"#405766","accent":"#e7c58c","wash":"#313021","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Archivo","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A shared treasury in a live battle","process":"Give each information layer a place","decisions":"From combat awareness to command","outcome":"The sea stays in view","reflection":"The next action needs an owner"},"decisionHeading":"Keep combat clear and command within reach.","resultHeading":"One arena, three layers of information."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain actual solo gameplay with compact battle score, hull status and persistent map","caption":"Actual local gameplay with an AI-filled crew: compact battle status, a persistent map, the Fleet control and the player’s hull.","label":"Actual solo gameplay","source":"Screenshot of the repository’s local built game on 16 October 2026; WebP delivery copy.","width":1280,"height":720,"focus":[{"title":"Keyboard and mouse","detail":"The playable prototype is designed around desktop sailing, aiming and command controls."},{"title":"Peer-to-peer scope","detail":"Rooms rely on a host and direct peer connections; solo AI-filled play remains a separate path."}],"details":[{"decision":1,"x":14,"y":484,"width":270,"height":222,"alt":"OhCaptain persistent corner map showing relays and fleet positions","caption":"The actual corner map keeps the wider objective available while the player sails."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"halo","name":"Halo","headline":"Keep the action close to the pointer."}},"halo":{"slug":"halo","name":"Halo","kind":"product","headline":"Keep the action close to the pointer.","summary":"A desktop action ring for frequently used shortcuts, with app-specific layouts and a visual editor.","role":"Interaction direction, action organisation & configuration UX","status":"Desktop utility · macOS and Windows","context":"Pointer-based shortcuts","facts":[{"value":"8","label":"slots in a ring"},{"value":"Per app","label":"different action sets"},{"value":"Click or flick","label":"two ways to choose"}],"problem":"A useful shortcut is one I can reach without breaking the task I am doing. Halo puts a ring of actions around the pointer, instead of asking me to hunt through menus or remember another keyboard combination. The design problem was keeping that first choice quick while still allowing the ring to grow.","process":["I organised the experience around opening the ring, choosing a direction and returning to the original task. Clicking supports a deliberate choice; holding the shortcut, moving towards an action and releasing supports a quicker gesture once the layout is familiar.","The editor exposes the same ring spatially. Selecting a slot opens its action, app behaviour, label and appearance settings. App-specific rings and folders hold more choices without putting every action into the first ring.","Halo is an independent project inspired by Logitech’s Actions Ring. I focused on the interaction and visual configuration. Comparing click and flick selection with familiar keyboard shortcuts is still a useful next test."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the first choice spatial","choice":"Keep actions in stable positions around the pointer, with labels available while choosing.","reason":"A direction can become familiar through use, while labels help someone who is still learning the layout.","tradeoff":"Moving actions frequently would work against that familiarity. Customisation needs to feel deliberate."},{"title":"Let the editor mirror the ring","choice":"Select a slot in the ring and edit that action beside it.","reason":"The person can see which position they are changing instead of translating between a list and the final layout.","tradeoff":"A visual editor still needs clear names and keyboard access; position alone is not enough."},{"title":"Keep specialised actions out of the first layer","choice":"Use app-specific rings and folders for actions that belong to a particular context.","reason":"A general ring can stay small while a specialist workflow has its own useful choices.","tradeoff":"Context changes and nested folders must remain predictable. More actions do not automatically make the tool faster."}],"outcome":"I gave the ring and its editor the same spatial model, with click and hold-and-flick selection, per-app configurations and nested action folders. The app supports actions such as app control, media, window management and snippets. Some actions depend on the operating system and the permissions available.","reflection":"I would start a comparison with a small, familiar task set rather than a ring full of possibilities. I want to know which positions people remember, when they hesitate and whether app-specific rings help or surprise them.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: configure three frequently used actions, repeat the task after a break and compare hesitation with an existing shortcut method.","Proposed checks: keyboard-only configuration, ring changes between apps, folder backtracking and permission-denied actions."],"sources":[{"label":"Halo README · using and customising the ring","detail":"Click/flick selection, app-specific rings, folders, platform support and permission boundaries. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Original editor and ring documentation images","detail":"Action-Ring/docs/images/editor.png and classic.png. Existing project graphics, not newly generated interface concepts. The ring example uses a neutral illustrated background."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-editor.webp","alt":"Halo original ring editor with a selected Finder action and its settings","caption":"Original Halo editor capture from the project documentation. The action’s position and settings are visible together.","width":1100,"height":740},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-ring.webp","alt":"Halo ring example with a labelled Play Pause action over a neutral illustrated page","caption":"Original ring example from the project documentation, shown over a neutral illustrated page rather than a live document.","width":720,"height":720}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/Halo","productActionLabel":"View Halo on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-editor.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":66,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Keep the action [[close to the pointer.]]","scene":"Imagine selecting text, opening an app and returning to the same document several times. A small set of nearby actions could keep that sequence from becoming a tour of menus.","question":"How much can a shortcut tool offer without making its first choice slower?","insight":"[[A small first ring needs a clear way to grow]]. Stable directions, app-specific contexts and folders serve different levels of familiarity.","pivot":"I kept the first ring focused and made the editor use the same positions that appear around the pointer.","takeaway":"A shortcut is useful when returning to the task feels easy.","journey":[{"title":"Open","detail":"Invoke the ring near the current pointer."},{"title":"Choose","detail":"Click a labelled action or use the hold-and-flick gesture."},{"title":"Configure","detail":"Select the same slot in the editor and adjust its action."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the first choice spatial","Let the editor mirror the ring","Keep specialised actions out of the first layer"],"annotations":[{"title":"Visual editor","detail":"The selected slot and its settings share one view."},{"title":"Platform scope","detail":"Some actions depend on OS capabilities or granted permissions."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"halo","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Desktop user with a few repeated actions","context":"Switching between a document, apps and small utility tasks.","need":"Reach a familiar action without a long interruption.","friction":"Too many shortcuts and contexts become another thing to remember.","implication":"Keep the first ring small, stable and easy to edit."}},"theme":{"layout":"halo","mode":"light","paper":"#f8f9fb","ink":"#202630","dim":"#596373","line":"#d4dae3","accent":"#315a9c","wash":"#e6edfa","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Instrument Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How much can a shortcut tool offer without making its first choice slower?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Halo","resultHeading":"A ring that stays connected to its editor."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/halo-editor.webp","alt":"Halo original ring editor with a selected Finder action and its settings","caption":"Original Halo editor capture from the project documentation. The action’s position and settings are visible together.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Action-Ring/docs/images/editor.png and classic.png. Existing project graphics, not newly generated interface concepts. The ring example uses a neutral illustrated background.","width":1100,"height":740},"nextStory":{"slug":"binnder","name":"Binnder","headline":"Review the file. Keep a way back."}},"binnder":{"slug":"binnder","name":"Binnder","kind":"product","headline":"Review the file. Keep a way back.","summary":"A native Android file-review app that combines large previews with explicit keep, bin and permanent-delete actions.","role":"File-review flow, destructive-action UX & information hierarchy","status":"Native Android app · early release","context":"Storage review","facts":[{"value":"5 seconds","label":"undo window"},{"value":"30 days","label":"bin retention"},{"value":"7 days","label":"before kept files return"}],"problem":"Storage cleanup is easy to rush and difficult to undo. I wanted the file itself to be the main thing on screen, with a clear difference between keeping it, moving it to a bin and deleting it permanently. The interaction should make those consequences visible before a gesture becomes an operation.","process":["I put the largest files first and used a large preview with fixed actions. File-type filters help narrow the queue, while matching buttons and accessibility actions make swiping optional.","I separated reversible binning from permanent deletion. An upward swipe asks for confirmation, and every deletion action has a five-second Undo window before the operation begins. A kept file is hidden from review for seven days rather than treated as permanently resolved.","The bin reports its retention period and supports preview and restore. Moving a file there does not free storage, so the interface must not count those bytes as recovered space. The captures use sample portfolio files in a separate emulator, not a scan of someone’s phone."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the file visible while deciding","choice":"Use a large preview, file name, size and fixed Keep, Bin and Delete forever controls.","reason":"The decision should be about a recognisable file, not an unfamiliar path in a long list.","tradeoff":"Some formats need another installed app. The preview must explain that limitation without implying a file is broken."},{"title":"Give permanent deletion its own commitment","choice":"Ask for confirmation and name the file before queuing permanent deletion, then offer the individual Undo window.","reason":"A gesture alone is too easy to misinterpret when the result cannot be recovered through the app.","tradeoff":"Extra confirmation slows cleanup. It belongs to the more consequential action rather than every decision."},{"title":"Describe the bin honestly","choice":"Show retention and restoration separately from bytes actually deleted.","reason":"A recoverable file still occupies storage. The person needs to understand both recovery and capacity.","tradeoff":"Retention depends on the device being able to run cleanup and reach the volume; it is not an exact deletion appointment."}],"outcome":"I designed one-file-at-a-time review with visible alternatives to gestures, a recoverable bin and an explicit permanent-delete path. The native app also accounts for interrupted operations and changed files. I still need to check those recovery paths across Android devices and removable storage.","reflection":"I would test a mistaken swipe, a removed storage volume and a restore destination that already exists. A cleanup tool earns trust when it explains what happened and preserves a way to recover, especially when it cannot finish an operation.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: review a small set of test files, undo an unintended action and restore a file without coaching.","Proposed checks: permission loss, app restart during a pending action, missing SD card and a file changed since scanning."],"sources":[{"label":"Binnder README and ReviewPolicy/Storage/SafeDisk source","detail":"Documented five-second Undo, seven-day keep, thirty-day bin, delayed operations, file identity checks and storage-accounting limits. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Native Android captures · isolated test files","detail":"Captured from Binnder 0.1.1 in a separate Android emulator. The displayed design deck is a sample copy of an existing portfolio PDF; no personal phone files were read."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-review.webp","alt":"Binnder native review screen showing a sample design PDF and keep bin and delete actions","caption":"Native Android capture using a sample portfolio PDF in an isolated emulator. Fixed controls provide the same actions as swiping.","width":1080,"height":1920},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-confirmation.webp","alt":"Binnder native permanent-deletion confirmation naming the sample file and Undo period","caption":"The actual confirmation names the sample file, explains that the bin is skipped and describes the five-second Undo window. No deletion was confirmed for this capture.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/Binnder","productActionLabel":"View Binnder on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-review.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":69,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Review the file. [[Keep a way back.]]","scene":"Imagine swiping through large downloads and realising that the last gesture targeted the wrong file. The interface needs to answer what happened and whether it can be reversed.","question":"How can a quick review still make destructive actions clear?","insight":"[[Keep, bin and permanent deletion are different decisions]]. Their consequences need different feedback, not just different swipe directions.","pivot":"I gave each action an explicit control and kept the file, consequence and recovery route close together.","takeaway":"The recovery path is part of the cleanup flow.","journey":[{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Preview the largest remaining file and check its details."},{"title":"Decide","detail":"Keep it, bin it or confirm permanent deletion."},{"title":"Recover","detail":"Use Undo while pending or restore from the bin."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the file visible while deciding","Give permanent deletion its own commitment","Describe the bin honestly"],"annotations":[{"title":"Sample files","detail":"The images show an isolated test queue, not personal storage."},{"title":"Space accounting","detail":"Binned files remain on disk until permanently deleted."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"binnder","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android user reviewing shared-storage files","context":"Working through a large-file queue and deciding what to keep.","need":"Understand the file and the consequence before committing.","friction":"Fast gestures can hide the difference between temporary and permanent removal.","implication":"Keep previews and fixed controls visible, with clear recovery states."}},"theme":{"layout":"binnder","mode":"light","paper":"#f9f8ff","ink":"#20212c","dim":"#5b5c6e","line":"#d8d7e6","accent":"#4c5f98","wash":"#e7e9fa","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How can a quick review still make destructive actions clear?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Binnder","resultHeading":"A review flow with an explicit recovery path."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/binnder-review.webp","alt":"Binnder native review screen showing a sample design PDF and keep bin and delete actions","caption":"Native Android capture using a sample portfolio PDF in an isolated emulator. Fixed controls provide the same actions as swiping.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from Binnder 0.1.1 in a separate Android emulator. The displayed design deck is a sample copy of an existing portfolio PDF; no personal phone files were read.","width":1080,"height":1920},"nextStory":{"slug":"pureeq","name":"PureEQ","headline":"Find a sound you can adjust and keep."}},"pureeq":{"slug":"pureeq","name":"PureEQ","kind":"product","headline":"Find a sound you can adjust and keep.","summary":"An Android sound-tuning app that combines a per-ear listening check, music preferences and editable EQ profiles.","role":"Setup journey, listening-test interaction & profile controls","status":"Native Android app","context":"Personal audio tuning","facts":[{"value":"Per ear","label":"separate listening input"},{"value":"A/B","label":"music preference choices"},{"value":"Export","label":"take the profile elsewhere"}],"problem":"A row of equaliser sliders assumes that someone already knows which frequencies they want to change. I approached PureEQ as a guided starting point: explain the setup, gather a few listening responses, compare music and leave the result open to adjustment.","process":["I separated the listening check from the taste test. The first asks whether a short tone is audible through the current headphones; the second asks which of two music treatments the person prefers. Neither needs to be presented as an expert diagnosis.","Preparation is part of the flow: a quiet environment, suitable headphones, stable volume and a left/right check. The result can then be adjusted with named controls, compared with EQ off and saved as a profile.","Export gives the profile a useful life beyond the setup. The documented formats have different capabilities, so a combined curve or per-ear adjustment needs explanation. The captures show the actual native starting flow, not a completed hearing test or a clinical result."],"decisions":[{"title":"Explain the two kinds of input","choice":"Describe listening responses and music preferences as separate steps before asking for either.","reason":"A person should know whether they are reporting audibility or choosing a sound they like.","tradeoff":"A short explanation must not make the listening check sound like a calibrated audiogram."},{"title":"Leave the suggestion editable","choice":"Offer named fine-tuning controls, an EQ on/off comparison and a way back to suggested settings.","reason":"The suggested curve is a starting point. People need to hear and revise it rather than accept it as an unquestionable result.","tradeoff":"Several controls can become confusing together. Reset and suggested-value feedback need to make changes traceable."},{"title":"Carry context into export","choice":"Describe the target format and explain when an app combines left and right curves.","reason":"A technically valid export can still behave differently from the per-ear result shown in PureEQ.","tradeoff":"Compatibility depends on the receiving app and device. Exporting a file does not guarantee the same playback everywhere."}],"outcome":"I connected preparation, listening responses, A/B choices and an editable result into a profile flow. The app can apply the EQ or export it for another player, with limitations explained for each format. This is personal sound tuning, not a clinical hearing assessment.","reflection":"I would test whether people can tell what came from their listening responses, what came from preference choices and what they changed afterwards. If those differences are unclear, the final curve may look more certain than it should.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: complete setup with headphones, explain each stage and compare a saved result with EQ off at matched loudness.","Proposed checks: interrupted setup, a volume change, incorrect channel identification, resetting one adjustment and exporting a combined curve."],"sources":[{"label":"PureEQ README and native UI/theme source","detail":"Listening and preference stages, preparation, editable profiles, reset behaviour and export limitations. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Native Android captures · no listening result","detail":"Captured from PureEQ 1.5.2 in a separate Android emulator. The welcome and preparation screens are genuine app UI; no human listening responses or clinical measurements are represented."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-start.webp","alt":"PureEQ native welcome screen explaining its listening and music preference flow","caption":"Actual native welcome screen from an isolated Android emulator. It explains the two inputs before a profile is created.","width":1080,"height":1920},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-setup.webp","alt":"PureEQ native new-profile preparation screen explaining listening taste and export","caption":"The actual preparation screen separates listening, taste and the resulting EQ. No hearing-test result is shown.","width":1080,"height":1920}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/PureEQ","productActionLabel":"View PureEQ on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-start.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":72,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Find a sound [[you can adjust and keep.]]","scene":"Imagine wanting clearer music but not knowing whether to change bass, treble or one particular band. A guided starting point needs to be understandable before the sliders become useful.","question":"How can sound tuning be guided without making its result feel final?","insight":"[[Audibility and preference answer different questions]]. The flow should explain that difference and leave the suggestion open to revision.","pivot":"I separated preparation, listening and taste, then kept comparison, adjustment and reset available around the result.","takeaway":"The person should understand the suggestion well enough to change it.","journey":[{"title":"Prepare","detail":"Check headphones, environment, volume and channels."},{"title":"Listen and compare","detail":"Report tones, then choose between music treatments."},{"title":"Adjust and keep","detail":"Compare, fine-tune, save or export the profile."}],"decisionLeads":["Explain the two kinds of input","Leave the suggestion editable","Carry context into export"],"annotations":[{"title":"Actual setup","detail":"The screenshots show preparation, not fabricated test results."},{"title":"Non-clinical scope","detail":"The personal tuning process is not a medical hearing assessment."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"pureeq","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android listener tuning a pair of headphones","context":"Looking for a guided starting point rather than editing frequency bands unaided.","need":"Understand the inputs and retain control over the sound.","friction":"A generated curve can look like an objective verdict.","implication":"Explain the stages and keep comparison and reset close to adjustments."}},"theme":{"layout":"pureeq","mode":"light","paper":"#f7f7fd","ink":"#181b28","dim":"#575d70","line":"#d9dcea","accent":"#5545c5","wash":"#e9e6fb","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How can sound tuning be guided without making its result feel final?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind PureEQ","resultHeading":"A guided suggestion that stays open to adjustment."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/pureeq-start.webp","alt":"PureEQ native welcome screen explaining its listening and music preference flow","caption":"Actual native welcome screen from an isolated Android emulator. It explains the two inputs before a profile is created.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from PureEQ 1.5.2 in a separate Android emulator. The welcome and preparation screens are genuine app UI; no human listening responses or clinical measurements are represented.","width":1080,"height":1920},"nextStory":{"slug":"hidden-identity-chess","name":"Hidden Identity Chess","headline":"Familiar chess. Very different information."}},"hidden-identity-chess":{"slug":"hidden-identity-chess","name":"Hidden Identity Chess","kind":"product","headline":"Familiar chess. Very different information.","summary":"A digital adaptation of Hidden Identity Chess, with private setup, bluffing, challenges and a focused play surface.","role":"Digital adaptation, private setup & game interaction design","status":"Playable web game","context":"Independent adaptation · Triple S Games rules","facts":[{"value":"Private","label":"opponent identities stay hidden"},{"value":"3 modes","label":"computer, shared device and online"},{"value":"Move / bluff / challenge","label":"explicit turn actions"}],"problem":"A familiar chessboard is useful orientation, but it can also create the wrong expectations when piece identities are hidden and bluffing is allowed. I focused the adaptation on explaining what is private, what a player can do now and how a challenge changes the game.","process":["I separated arranging an army from playing it. In same-device mode, a covered handoff protects the next player’s view. Online play needs the same distinction in what each player receives, not just a visual cover over shared data.","Zen mode keeps the board, players, turn and Move, Bluff and Challenge controls in view. History, hints and secondary tools move into sheets so they can be inspected without permanently shrinking the board.","The rules and original game concept are credited to Triple S Games. This is an independent digital adaptation. I treated computer play, shared-device play and online rooms as separate flows because they have different privacy and connection requirements."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make hidden information explicit","choice":"Show the player’s own identities and keep the opponent’s pieces visually hidden.","reason":"Someone should understand the information available to each side before making a move.","tradeoff":"Shared-device privacy depends on a clear handoff; it cannot stop someone looking at the screen at the wrong time."},{"title":"Name the unusual actions","choice":"Keep Move, Bluff and Challenge as separate controls with rules available nearby.","reason":"The familiar board should not make a new player assume ordinary check-and-checkmate behaviour.","tradeoff":"A challenge can refer to earlier play, so its target and consequence need careful explanation."},{"title":"Give the board the space","choice":"Use the focused play view and move history and secondary tools into deliberate inspection surfaces.","reason":"The board is easier to read when optional tools do not compete for its space.","tradeoff":"Hiding history can make challenges harder to investigate. The route back to a past move must remain obvious."}],"outcome":"I designed setup, private handoff and the focused game view around the information each player can use. The game supports computer, same-device and online play, with explicit challenge controls and local saving. I still want to test whether a new player understands the handoff and can investigate a challenge.","reflection":"I would watch where an experienced chess player applies an ordinary rule that does not belong here. Those moments would tell me where the adaptation needs a clearer explanation rather than another hint.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: arrange an army, make a bluff and investigate a past move with a first-time player.","Proposed checks: shared-device handoff, keyboard square navigation, a disconnected online player and restoring a local game."],"sources":[{"label":"Project README and original rules credit","detail":"Private setup, computer/shared-device/online modes, focused play, challenge handling and credit to Triple S Games. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Actual local game captures","detail":"Captured from the local built game under /game/ in computer mode. Positions illustrate interaction states; no human playtest result is represented."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-board.webp","alt":"Hidden Identity Chess actual board with visible own pieces and hidden opponent identities","caption":"Actual local computer-mode interface. The opponent’s identities remain hidden while the player’s own army is visible.","width":1275,"height":766},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-setup.webp","alt":"Hidden Identity Chess army arrangement screen and mode choices","caption":"Actual setup screen with army arrangement and the separate computer, online and shared-device modes.","width":1275,"height":1102}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hidden-identity-chess-club.soundmite3.chatgpt.site/game/","productActionLabel":"Play Hidden Identity Chess","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-board.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":75,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Familiar chess. [[Very different information.]]","scene":"Imagine knowing how a knight moves but not knowing which enemy piece is a knight. The board is familiar; the information behind the decision is not.","question":"How do familiar controls explain a game with unfamiliar information?","insight":"[[The board alone cannot explain the rules]]. Setup, hidden identities and the three turn actions need to be visible in the interaction.","pivot":"I kept the board central and made the difference between moving, bluffing and challenging explicit.","takeaway":"Familiarity should help someone learn, not conceal a different rule.","journey":[{"title":"Arrange","detail":"Place an army privately before the game."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Choose a move or a bluff with the visible information."},{"title":"Investigate","detail":"Use history and a challenge when a move needs scrutiny."}],"decisionLeads":["Make hidden information explicit","Name the unusual actions","Give the board the space"],"annotations":[{"title":"Independent adaptation","detail":"The original concept and rules are credited to Triple S Games."},{"title":"Actual computer mode","detail":"The captures show the app, not a fabricated multiplayer session."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"hidden-identity-chess","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Chess player learning a hidden-information variant","context":"Recognising the board while learning different movement and challenge expectations.","need":"Understand which information is private and which action is available.","friction":"Ordinary chess habits can conceal the rules that changed.","implication":"Name the unusual actions and keep their consequences inspectable."}},"theme":{"layout":"hidden-identity-chess","mode":"light","paper":"#f6f7f2","ink":"#193b35","dim":"#586e66","line":"#dce2d8","accent":"#265b4d","wash":"#e8ead3","bodyFont":"Manrope","displayFont":"Manrope","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How do familiar controls explain a game with unfamiliar information?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Hidden Identity Chess","resultHeading":"A familiar board with clear private information."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/hidden-chess-board.webp","alt":"Hidden Identity Chess actual board with visible own pieces and hidden opponent identities","caption":"Actual local computer-mode interface. The opponent’s identities remain hidden while the player’s own army is visible.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from the local built game under /game/ in computer mode. Positions illustrate interaction states; no human playtest result is represented.","width":1275,"height":766},"nextStory":{"slug":"five-dead","name":"5 Dead","headline":"Keep the table moving. Keep the turn clear."}},"five-dead":{"slug":"five-dead","name":"5 Dead","kind":"product","headline":"Keep the table moving. Keep the turn clear.","summary":"An independent digital card game with bot play, shared-device turns and online rooms, built around a visible running total and card actions.","role":"Game presentation, joining flow & turn feedback","status":"Playable card game","context":"Independent game inspired by 5 Alive","facts":[{"value":"21","label":"safe running-total limit"},{"value":"3 modes","label":"bots, pass-and-play and online"},{"value":"2–24","label":"extended party-table scope"}],"problem":"A card table has several things to explain at once: the current total, whose turn it is, what a card will do and who is still in the hand. I wanted the presentation to give each moment a clear focus without making players work through a rules panel on every turn.","process":["I kept the running total and current discard near the centre, opponents around the table and the local hand close to the player. Selection and a Play action help separate considering a card from committing it.","The three entry modes make different promises. Bot play and local pass-and-play can work offline after the app is ready; online rooms depend on a connection and wait for a disconnected player. Shared-device turns use a privacy screen between hands.","This is an independent game inspired by Hasbro’s 5 Alive. Larger tables use a disclosed house rule. The generated arena and card artwork are assets used in the game itself; I kept the running total, hand and turn feedback distinct from that background."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the running total central","choice":"Place the total, discard and turn state where the next decision is made.","reason":"A player needs to connect the chosen card to the current state of the hand.","tradeoff":"Effects and animations can obscure that relationship if they linger or hide the next turn."},{"title":"Separate choosing from playing","choice":"Show a selected card and an explicit Play action, alongside the supported drag interaction.","reason":"Considering a card should not accidentally commit it, especially on a crowded phone hand.","tradeoff":"An extra action can slow experienced play. Both methods need clear feedback and usable touch targets."},{"title":"Explain the mode before the table starts","choice":"Distinguish bot, pass-and-play and connected rooms, including their waiting and privacy states.","reason":"A group should know whether it needs one device, several devices or a network before starting.","tradeoff":"Local privacy covers are a social convention; online connection loss can pause the table."}],"outcome":"I brought the hand, table, turn feedback and joining flow into one playable presentation. Bot and local play work separately from connected rooms. Larger tables follow the documented house rules, with waiting and privacy states suited to each mode.","reflection":"I would test the smallest phone hand and the largest table before adding more effects. The useful questions are whether the next player notices their turn and whether someone can tell what a special card just changed.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: start a bot hand, play a special card and explain the next action without opening the full rules.","Proposed checks: overlapping cards, shared-device handoff, a disconnected seat and the clarity of the extended-table rule."],"sources":[{"label":"5 Dead README · modes, rules and extended tables","detail":"Mode boundaries, the 21 limit, hand privacy, online waiting and the disclosed larger-table variant. Reviewed 31 October 2026. Exact deck counts for a particular physical edition have not been verified."},{"label":"Original game captures and DESIGN.md","detail":"Existing outputs/5dead-arena-v2.png and 5dead-card-faces-phone.png. These show the actual game presentation, including its generated arena, avatar and card art."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-arena.webp","alt":"5 Dead actual game table with running total opponents and a selected card","caption":"Original game capture. The arena, avatars and skull card art are generated assets used by the playable game; labels and controls are part of the actual interface.","width":844,"height":390},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-hand.webp","alt":"5 Dead original phone layout showing special cards and the Play control","caption":"Original game capture showing the phone hand, special cards and the Play control.","width":915,"height":412}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/5Dead","productActionLabel":"View 5 Dead on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-arena.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":78,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Keep the table moving. [[Keep the turn clear.]]","scene":"Imagine a special card changing the direction of play just as an animation finishes. Everyone needs to know what changed and who acts next.","question":"What does the next player need to see before choosing a card?","insight":"[[The state change matters more than the effect]]. Total, turn and the selected card need to remain understandable through the animation.","pivot":"I gave the running total a stable place and kept card selection, commitment and turn feedback distinct.","takeaway":"An effect has done its job when the table knows what happens next.","journey":[{"title":"Choose a table","detail":"Select bots, shared-device play or an online room."},{"title":"Read the turn","detail":"Check the total, discard and available hand."},{"title":"Play and continue","detail":"Commit a card and follow its effect into the next turn."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the running total central","Separate choosing from playing","Explain the mode before the table starts"],"annotations":[{"title":"Actual game artwork","detail":"Generated arena and card art appear in the game itself and are identified as such."},{"title":"Disclosed variant","detail":"More-than-six-player tables use documented house rules."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"five-dead","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Player joining a short card game","context":"Learning the digital table and special-card feedback, possibly on a phone.","need":"Know the current total, available action and next turn.","friction":"Crowded hands and effects can hide a simple decision.","implication":"Keep core state stable and use clear commitment feedback."}},"theme":{"layout":"five-dead","mode":"dark","paper":"#101d31","ink":"#f6f4ec","dim":"#b9c6dc","line":"#435675","accent":"#ffd084","wash":"#3b3021","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Barlow Condensed","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"What does the next player need to see before choosing a card?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind 5 Dead","resultHeading":"A table with a clear total and a clear next turn."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/five-dead-arena.webp","alt":"5 Dead actual game table with running total opponents and a selected card","caption":"Original game capture. The arena, avatars and skull card art are generated assets used by the playable game; labels and controls are part of the actual interface.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Existing outputs/5dead-arena-v2.png and 5dead-card-faces-phone.png. These show the actual game presentation, including its generated arena, avatar and card art.","width":844,"height":390},"nextStory":{"slug":"alibi","name":"Alibi","headline":"Watch privately. Give just enough away."}},"alibi":{"slug":"alibi","name":"Alibi","kind":"product","headline":"Watch privately. Give just enough away.","summary":"A social video-imposter game that takes a group through private clips, one-word clues, discussion, voting and a reveal.","role":"Game direction, private-viewing flow & group interaction design","status":"Playable browser game","context":"Social deduction · 3–8 players","facts":[{"value":"3–8","label":"players in a room"},{"value":"One word","label":"clue from each player"},{"value":"Private","label":"clips and votes before reveal"}],"problem":"A social-deduction game loses its rhythm when someone misses a step or sees information too soon. Alibi gives most players the same clip and one player a related alternative, then moves the group through clues, discussion and voting. The flow needs to protect the mystery without making the group wait without an explanation.","process":["I structured each round as private viewing, one-word submission, shared clues, discussion, a hidden vote and the reveal. Players need a shared phase and a private view at the same time.","I used a solo practice path with clearly labelled simulated opponents so the whole sequence can be tried before inviting friends. A practice lobby should not look like proof that other people are connected.","The project includes room chat and built-in calling, with external calls as another option. Playback depends on YouTube availability and embedding, and the server keeps room state in memory. Those limits make readiness, replacement clips and host recovery controls part of the design."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep private viewing separate from the group phase","choice":"Show each assigned clip privately and release clues together after submission or timeout.","reason":"Early clues or role information could change the game before everyone has had a chance to act.","tradeoff":"A shared phase still needs a clear explanation when a player is loading or has not submitted."},{"title":"Make practice an honest invitation","choice":"Label simulated players throughout solo practice and let the person try the real sequence.","reason":"Someone can learn the rhythm without assembling a group or mistaking demo activity for real participants.","tradeoff":"Practice can explain controls but cannot reproduce the conversation and hesitation of a group."},{"title":"Explain waits and recovery","choice":"Keep the current phase, readiness and host controls visible when playback or connection needs attention.","reason":"The group needs a next action rather than an unexplained delay.","tradeoff":"A replacement clip or returning player can affect the round. The interface needs to say when play restarts instead of hiding that change."}],"outcome":"I connected the lobby, private watching, clues, discussion, votes and reveal into one group flow. Solo practice explains the steps with simulated opponents; connected rooms support real players. A study with a real group is the next step for evaluating the social experience.","reflection":"I would watch a real group through a failed clip and a late player, not just a successful reveal. Those pauses would show whether the instructions preserve the social rhythm or turn the host into technical support.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: run a short group session and observe phase explanations, accidental information sharing and waiting time.","Proposed checks: a blocked YouTube embed, a reconnecting player, a departing host and switching between built-in and external calling."],"sources":[{"label":"Alibi README and round-state source","detail":"Private assignments, simultaneous clues, secret votes, practice labelling, playback and connection limits. Reviewed 31 October 2026."},{"label":"Actual browser captures · solo practice","detail":"Captured from the local VideoImposter application. The practice capture uses its clearly labelled simulated opponents, with no camera, microphone or personal-call recording."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-home.webp","alt":"Alibi actual landing interface explaining its video-imposter game","caption":"Actual local browser interface. The five-player diagram explains the rules before a room starts.","width":1275,"height":717},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-practice.webp","alt":"Alibi actual solo-practice lobby with clearly labelled simulated players","caption":"Actual solo-practice lobby with clearly labelled simulated opponents.","width":1275,"height":717}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/VideoImposter","productActionLabel":"View Alibi on GitHub","productHeaderLabel":"View project","cover":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-home.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":81,"pdfVersion":"9b2b96b5361e","narrative":{"headline":"Watch privately. [[Give just enough away.]]","scene":"Imagine one person still watching while everyone else begins discussing their clue. The game needs to coordinate the phase without revealing which clip anyone received.","question":"How can a shared round protect information that belongs to one player?","insight":"[[Shared timing and private information need separate treatment]]. A visible phase can coordinate the room without exposing a clip or a vote.","pivot":"I made the round sequence explicit and kept solo practice visibly different from a room of real players.","takeaway":"A clear next step leaves more room for the conversation.","journey":[{"title":"Enter","detail":"Create or join a room, or choose labelled solo practice."},{"title":"Watch and clue","detail":"See a private clip and submit one word."},{"title":"Discuss and reveal","detail":"Compare shared clues, vote privately and see the result."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep private viewing separate from the group phase","Make practice an honest invitation","Explain waits and recovery"],"annotations":[{"title":"Labelled practice","detail":"The capture includes simulated players rather than fabricated participation."},{"title":"Playback dependency","detail":"Clips use the official YouTube player and can be unavailable."}],"evidenceTag":"Original project and interface captures · reviewed October 2026","tone":"alibi","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Friend joining a social video game","context":"Entering a room and learning the round while keeping a clip private.","need":"Understand the phase and what can safely be shared.","friction":"Playback delays and unclear waits interrupt the group.","implication":"Show readiness, private boundaries and the host’s next available action."}},"theme":{"layout":"alibi","mode":"dark","paper":"#12141b","ink":"#f4f5fa","dim":"#b4c4da","line":"#3b4557","accent":"#b9f64a","wash":"#2b381e","bodyFont":"Manrope","displayFont":"Barlow Condensed","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"How can a shared round protect information that belongs to one player?","process":"What shaped the design","decisions":"The decisions","outcome":"The resulting experience","reflection":"What I would take forward"},"decisionHeading":"The choices behind Alibi","resultHeading":"A private clip inside a shared round."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/alibi-home.webp","alt":"Alibi actual landing interface explaining its video-imposter game","caption":"Actual local browser interface. The five-player diagram explains the rules before a room starts.","label":"Original project interface","source":"Captured from the local VideoImposter application. The practice capture uses its clearly labelled simulated opponents, with no camera, microphone or personal-call recording.","width":1275,"height":717},"nextStory":{"slug":"onespace","name":"OneSpace","headline":"Finding a class file should be straightforward."}}};
